WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.520
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies and&nbsp;
germs. This is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.640
episode of The Tim Ferriss Show. And I'm going to&nbsp;
skip the preamble. I'm going to keep it light. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:29.440
guest today, my oh, my, do we have lots to cover&nbsp;
and I'm excited to dig in. My guest today is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.920
Sheila Heen. This is her second appearance&nbsp;
on the podcast. Sheila has spent the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.960
three decades working to understand how people&nbsp;
can better navigate conflict with a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.320
specialty in difficult conversations. God knows&nbsp;
we need more of that expertise for all of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.480
sakes. She is a founder of Triad Consulting&nbsp;
Group, a professor at Harvard Law School,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.160
and a co-author of Thanks for the Feedback: The&nbsp;
Science and Art of Receiving Feedback Well (Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.360
When It Is off Base, Unfair, Poorly Delivered,&nbsp;
and Frankly, You’re Not in the Mood) with Douglas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:05.920
Stone and Difficult Conversations, subtitle, How&nbsp;
to Discuss What Matters Most. Also with Douglas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.920
Stone and Bruce Patton, with a newly updated&nbsp;
third edition that was just released in August.&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.160
Sheila and her colleagues at Triad work with&nbsp;
leaders and organizations to build their capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:19.960
to have the conversations that matter most. Her&nbsp;
clients have included Pixar, American Express,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:26.040
the NBA, the Singapore Supreme Court. Maybe we'll&nbsp;
talk about that. Who knows? The Obama White House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.840
and theologians struggling with the nature of&nbsp;
truth and God. She's schooled in negotiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.080
daily by her three children. You can find&nbsp;
my first and very popular conversation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:40.907
Sheila at tim.blog/sheilaheen. Sheila, nice&nbsp;
to see you again. Thanks for making the time.&nbsp;

00:01:40.907 --> 00:01:45.440
Sheila Heen: It's great to see you.
Tim Ferriss: I know last time we spoke at length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:54.760
I was in the midst of a very challenging chapter&nbsp;
in my personal relationship. And when my team and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.160
I reviewed the last conversation we had, which I&nbsp;
always do before a conversation like this, so went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:08.560
through the entire round one. And there was a line&nbsp;
that stood out for all of us. And it might be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.680
paraphrase. You can feel free to fact check this.
Sheila Heen: Yeah, yeah. I can't wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.520
to hear what you're about to say.
Tim Ferriss: Quote, "It's not just that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.640
difficult conversations in our most important&nbsp;
relationships. Those conversations are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:31.440
relationship." And I've been thinking about this&nbsp;
a lot in the sense that it seems like our most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:39.120
important relationships are defined and steered&nbsp;
in large part by how we handle the difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.600
conversations, how we handle things when we are&nbsp;
perhaps not our best selves. And to that extent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.840
they're what define a good or a bad relationship.&nbsp;
So I just wanted to share that, because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.240
stuck out for everyone who reviewed the last&nbsp;
conversation that we had on the side of my team.&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.600
How should we talk about — because we did discuss&nbsp;
a lot surrounding difficult conversations, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:08.907
will edge into a lot of shared territory.&nbsp;
But how should we talk about feedback?&nbsp;

00:03:08.907 --> 00:03:14.120
Sheila Heen: For me, when feedback is incoming,&nbsp;
part of what I'm doing is scanning for what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:20.920
wrong with it. And we can circle back to&nbsp;
this question because there's a great set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:26.240
of things that are typically wrong with the&nbsp;
feedback, at least that other people give me.&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.960
So it's a shift from I have to decide whether&nbsp;
I agree or disagree, and what's wrong with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:39.680
to before I decide, let me just understand it.&nbsp;
Let me just understand what they're trying to say.&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:47.880
So if we go to, what language does that suggest?&nbsp;
You can ask questions in one of two directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:55.080
Feedback always has a past and a future. So you&nbsp;
can either ask more about where it's coming from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:02.400
like, "Say more about what you heard me say,"&nbsp;
or, "Can you give me an example of that?" or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.640
"Tell me more about the impact that had in&nbsp;
the exchange." So you're asking questions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:14.280
curiosity about, "What is it that you notice&nbsp;
that is prompting you to say this to me?"&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.760
The other direction that you can&nbsp;
ask questions in is forward-looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.320
where is it going to. "If I were to take your&nbsp;
advice, what would I do differently? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:29.680
specifically are you suggesting or requesting?"
If I'm feeling particularly defensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.920
that second set of questions is sometimes&nbsp;
easier. If I ask the backward-looking ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.560
it'll suck me into an argument about&nbsp;
why they misunderstood, or misremember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:48.640
or that's not really what happened. If I just&nbsp;
go to, "Okay, if I were to follow your advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:55.680
what would that look like in practice, in everyday&nbsp;
life?" And sometimes they have an instant reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:05.440
and I realize, you're just saying — drop you&nbsp;
a note back to say, "I got it. I can do that."&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:12.040
If I thought, what we were talking about, "Am&nbsp;
I responsive in a fully human emotional way?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.920
you're just saying, "Just let me know whether&nbsp;
you got my note. You don't have to give me the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.280
whole answer." Which is of course why maybe I'm&nbsp;
not responding yet because I'm like, "Oh, gosh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.120
that's a big question. Let me think about it.&nbsp;
I want to give you a good response." And you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.960
just saying, "Just let know you got it. You&nbsp;
can give the full response later." So that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:35.200
an example of, "I now understand what you want."
Sometimes the person doesn't know what they want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.400
but then that centers the conversation on, "Okay,&nbsp;
so I don't know how to change if you don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:46.200
what would tell you that this is better."
Tim Ferriss: Right. There's phrasing from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:55.840
the last conversation we had that came up multiple&nbsp;
times, which was, "Help me understand…" which I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:08.440
thought was very skillful because it is basically&nbsp;
indirectly pointing the fingers at yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:17.240
if that makes sense. Rather than "Explain&nbsp;
yourself," "Help me understand" implies a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:23.960
level of responsibility on the receiver that might&nbsp;
help to diffuse things or deescalate things. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:32.160
someone giving feedback comes in hot, so the&nbsp;
wording might be diplomatic, but the tone is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.560
"Listen, fucker, A, B, or C?"
Sheila Heen: Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:40.800
it's amazing how clear that message is.
Tim Ferriss: Any thoughts for either how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:48.640
to deescalate or self-talk that you can use so&nbsp;
that the person on the receiving end is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:57.520
reply in a calm, cool, and collected fashion?
Sheila Heen: It could be that calm, cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:06.000
and collected is more aspirational than realistic.&nbsp;
And one thing that can help is just to name the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:14.640
surprise, or the, this just came out of left&nbsp;
field, I'm totally knocked off my center. So just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:26.080
saying, "Wow, okay. Sorry, this is coming out of&nbsp;
left field, so I'm just kind of on my back foot,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:32.320
so that you're just naming where you're at in this&nbsp;
moment. And sometimes, just naming it can help you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.400
find your feet again because you're like, "Okay,&nbsp;
I've just accurately described where I'm at. I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:45.520
be in that space," and now they know I'm in that&nbsp;
space. Or just saying, "Okay, I'm feeling a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:55.200
defensive." And then you can move to a question.
A question that I've been using recently that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:01.080
helped quickly get to the heart of what's going on&nbsp;
is, "What do you feel like I don't get? What is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:07.600
that you feel I really don't get about whatever?"&nbsp;
This situation, about how this impacted you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:16.440
whatever they're trying to tell you that&nbsp;
they feel like you're really clueless about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:24.720
that's often going to cut through the noise.
Tim Ferriss: So let's, if we could,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:37.980
just jump into a real-life example. And perhaps&nbsp;
we could examine painful feedback from the past.&nbsp;

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:41.720
Sheila Heen: Your own?
Tim Ferriss: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:50.787
we can talk about my own. But I thought we would,&nbsp;
given my job as the inquisitor, I would ask —&nbsp;

00:08:50.787 --> 00:08:54.080
Sheila Heen: That will not protect you, my friend.
Tim Ferriss: I know. It's not the flak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:55.720
jacket I always hope it to be.
Sheila Heen: I know. I know.&nbsp;

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.800
Tim Ferriss: I came into this expecting that&nbsp;
I would be opening the kimono per se, but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:07.160
just finished the third edition of Difficult&nbsp;
Conversations, and you had to contend with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:16.107
reader feedback on an example in that book that&nbsp;
got large reactions. Could you elaborate on this?&nbsp;

00:09:16.107 --> 00:09:22.160
Sheila Heen: So there were a lot of things that&nbsp;
we looked at fresh, and rewrote, and reworked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:30.040
and changed about the third edition. And one&nbsp;
of the examples that got the biggest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:37.360
consistent negative reaction from readers&nbsp;
was an example, a story in the chapter about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:43.680
shifting from blame to joint contribution.
And it's a sexual harassment example. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:50.600
although it's told to characters, because all&nbsp;
the examples in the book are real or amalgams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:57.840
but disguised. That particular&nbsp;
example was mine, and it came from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:05.320
experience that I had pretty early in my career.
I was in my twenties, I was sent abroad to work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:11.280
a project, international project. It was bringing&nbsp;
together consultants from lots of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:18.080
places. And simply because my organization was&nbsp;
the one that was holding the funding and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:24.480
charge of the project in the collaboration,&nbsp;
I got named as the team lead. And I was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:29.440
youngest person on the team by a good margin.
And so part of what happened, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:38.280
and we're in a very stressful context on site&nbsp;
in a divided ethnic conflict. So the task that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:42.400
we have together, we have to come together,&nbsp;
build a set of team relationships that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:49.520
highly functional and adaptive in the moment as&nbsp;
things unfold. And so the first couple of days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:55.120
are really spent sorting out, how do we connect&nbsp;
and how are we going to collaborate together as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:00.760
we tackle getting ready for the project?
Over those first couple of days, things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:05.440
settled down in terms of people's reactions to me&nbsp;
being the team lead. I'm sure their first reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:12.680
was like, "Really? Because you look like you're&nbsp;
still in high school." And I had several decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:19.160
of experience, which was fair enough, honestly.
There was one guy who was particularly hostile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:24.400
to the idea and continued to be hostile to&nbsp;
the idea. So I decide that the answer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:32.240
this to win him over and sort out whatever's&nbsp;
going on with his hostility to my leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:38.200
I'll assign us a piece of the next day to work on&nbsp;
together. And then we'll just have to sort it out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:45.000
right? Because we'll work on it one-on-one.
So I did that and it worked beautifully. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:52.480
soon learned that it had worked a little too&nbsp;
well, because he started saying things like, "I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:12:01.240
feel like in another world we would've fallen in&nbsp;
love." He was indicating, directly and indirectly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:10.840
romantic interest. And I was completely freaked&nbsp;
out by that. And so I'd say, "I'm married." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:18.000
he said, "So am I. What's the problem?"
And oddly enough, I felt totally paralyzed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.760
by it. And I'm not a shy person. I'm pretty quick&nbsp;
to speak up. In fact, that's some of the feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.000
I'm getting now, which is I'm a little too&nbsp;
quick to speak up. We can circle back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:35.640
that. Now I just have the opposite problem. And&nbsp;
I felt like I'm sending the indirect signals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:41.840
and slightly more direct signals that I'm not&nbsp;
interested, and it's not stopping. And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:52.680
trying to avoid him, etc. He's always right&nbsp;
there. At any meal, he's right next to me.&nbsp;

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:58.840
And so I felt paralyzed because I felt like,&nbsp;
"Gosh, I have to be sure that I'm right about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:03.640
intentions, that I'm reading him correctly. Maybe&nbsp;
it's just a cultural difference between us." There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:13.080
was no HR to go to, and it wasn't a hierarchical&nbsp;
thing. Some sexual harassment situations are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:19.840
really tough, because it's in hierarchy&nbsp;
with a boss. Here, I'm actually in charge.&nbsp;

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:26.800
So the insight for me was, "Oh, gosh, I don't&nbsp;
need to be sure I'm right about his intentions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:31.840
I just have to describe the impact that it's&nbsp;
having on me," number one. And number two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:35.200
and the reason it's in the contribution&nbsp;
chapter, is that it suddenly started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:40.440
occurring to me that I might have been sending&nbsp;
mixed signals by accident. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:46.680
I arranged for us to do this piece of the project&nbsp;
together, and maybe that was signaling interest.&nbsp;

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:53.840
And so if I just change my contribution —&nbsp;
so my contribution to the problem was maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.840 --> 00:14:01.600
inadvertently seeming like I was reaching out to&nbsp;
give him special attention, because I was paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:09.320
attention to what he was doing to try to get him&nbsp;
in our meetings, to get him engaged, win him over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:13.640
And the other thing I'm contributing is I'm not&nbsp;
saying directly that I'm not interested, so I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:22.000
change those contributions. So that was a big aha&nbsp;
for me. We disguise it, we write it into the book.&nbsp;

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:32.640
The reaction that we got was, we had one student&nbsp;
of ours who told us, "When I got to that part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:38.160
I threw the book across the room," which is&nbsp;
not really the reader reaction you're hoping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.080
for. I don't know about you.
Tim Ferriss: What was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:47.120
explanation behind that, if there was one?
Sheila Heen: "You're blaming the victim. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:54.480
blaming the victim. You are saying that she…" the&nbsp;
name in the book is Sydney, "...that Sydney is at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.480 --> 00:15:00.800
fault for her own situation, that she should&nbsp;
deal with it alone. In other words, she's got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:09.520
to figure out what she needs to change, because&nbsp;
she got herself into this." And people also had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:14.080
strong views about what was really going on with&nbsp;
him. They had strong views about what you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:21.800
do. "You should never have this conversation&nbsp;
directly. It's unsafe. You should go to HR." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:28.600
it was a very clear set of themes in the reaction.&nbsp;
By the way, what's your reaction as you listen?&nbsp;

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:35.360
Tim Ferriss: My reaction as I listen&nbsp;
is actually more a set of questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:44.400
For instance, I'm very curious if the throw&nbsp;
the book across the room reaction is mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:50.920
American-born-and-raised readers of a certain&nbsp;
vintage. I'm wondering if that's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:57.880
you have observed in readers from many other&nbsp;
cultures. I'm also wondering many other ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:07.320
because it strikes me as though that may be a&nbsp;
very contemporary, industrialized US response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:11.800
or North American response to things, which&nbsp;
is just a hypothesis. But that's what comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:16.880
to mind from me from a curiosity perspective.
Sheila Heen: Totally. And what I particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:26.720
like about your curiosity is that like me, it&nbsp;
allows us to locate the problem in them. "It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:34.240
just that you guys are either…" and I have this&nbsp;
reaction, totally, "...too young…" Like I was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:38.600
"...you'll get it once you're in the work world."&nbsp;
A big reaction was like, "Well, this just can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:44.840
happen. This should not happen," which I don't&nbsp;
disagree with. And it does. So the goal is, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:51.760
do you do about it? So some people were upset that&nbsp;
it was even in the book, because you shouldn't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:55.600
talking about these things. It's like, "Well, it's&nbsp;
a book on difficult conversations. So we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:57.920
be tackling the ones that actually happen."
Tim Ferriss: We're not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:00.000
to choose the easy ones.
Sheila Heen: We're not going to choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.840
the easy ones. And if we don't have it, people&nbsp;
say, "Well, what about really hard situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:10.920
like sexual harassment? You don't address that."&nbsp;
So I agree with you that that reaction was coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:21.760
partly from — and it has morphed a little bit&nbsp;
over the years in terms of the generational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:28.400
sense of what should happen in the ideal world. I&nbsp;
think when we're in our twenties as young adults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:35.440
we come into the world with a strong sense of&nbsp;
the way the world should work. And then, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:41.200
discover that that's not the way the world works&nbsp;
in many situations. And it's kind of outrageous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:48.960
And then we're like, "Why are we not changing&nbsp;
this? Why are you guys tolerating it?" And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:58.920
is why revolutions are led by young people.
And I think as I've gotten older, what I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:04.400
noticing in myself is that okay, I address some&nbsp;
things and don't address others. But I also figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:11.160
out how to navigate the world as it is. And then&nbsp;
those of us in leadership have been successful in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:15.520
the world as it is. And so we're like, "Don't&nbsp;
worry about it. You'll be able to navigate."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:20.800
It's a lot better than what I was your age. But&nbsp;
I think that's partly why we get complacent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:25.800
because we do see the progress that's been made,&nbsp;
but then we can underappreciate the progress still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:34.440
to be made. I'll also say that I want to be&nbsp;
fair to the other readers, some of whom were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:44.120
young women from other cultures who have had even&nbsp;
more stressful, extreme, horrific experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:52.800
and were looking for guidance in feeling like&nbsp;
they were being told, "Well, you're on your own."&nbsp;

00:18:52.800 --> 00:19:02.360
Tim Ferriss: I was going to just say before&nbsp;
we unpack some of the macro, and also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:06.720
example that you're about to give, which I don't&nbsp;
want you to lose track of, unless I missed it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:12.960
you didn't share how you ultimately address the&nbsp;
situation. So I feel like that's a critical piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:20.720
of the story. So in the original edition,&nbsp;
let's just say, how did you explain how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:26.800
then handled that very uncomfortable situation?
Sheila Heen: So part of what was so powerful for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:31.200
me about realizing, "Actually, there are&nbsp;
a couple of things that I have done that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:36.960
have probably contributed to this. And those I&nbsp;
have control over, those I can change." And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:43.720
was a relief. That was liberating for me, was,&nbsp;
"Oh, okay, well I can be clear about the fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:48.280
even clearer about the fact. And I don't have&nbsp;
to know that I'm right about his intentions."&nbsp;

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:57.280
So I pulled him aside and said, "Hey, by the&nbsp;
way, I may not be reading you right at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:06.200
So I'm not saying this is what you intend.&nbsp;
And you have made a number of comments about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:10.880
having a romantic relationship or a personal&nbsp;
relationship. And I think I probably haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:16.120
been clear that that's not of interest to&nbsp;
me. I'm enjoying our working relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:26.120
and that's super fun, and I think we're doing a&nbsp;
great job. But if I've been unclear, I apologize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:31.560
for that." And that was kind of the end of it.
We only had another week or so of work to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:41.360
together, but I felt incredibly empowered. And&nbsp;
so therefore, when later readers are saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:46.600
"You're blaming the victim and you're saying&nbsp;
it's her fault and that she's on her own," I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:50.560
felt really misunderstood because I was like,&nbsp;
"No, no, no. We have a whole section of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:58.560
chapter that's about it's really important not to&nbsp;
blame the victim." And that a victim seeing some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:04.400
contribution also gives them back some control and&nbsp;
power sometimes. It's not saying they did anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:10.720
wrong, you're not blaming them. They didn't do&nbsp;
anything wrong. And it helps them have a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:17.400
bit of control over making sure it doesn't happen&nbsp;
again in many cases, which was how it felt to me.&nbsp;

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:26.880
So when we're getting this feedback from readers,&nbsp;
I lost sight of what we're trying to do in a book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:35.640
which is that we're trying to put in stories that&nbsp;
resonate with people, where people are — we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:41.600
of writing as a conversation with the reader's&nbsp;
internal voice. And if we're doing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:46.880
that's creating a, "Wait a minute, that's not&nbsp;
true. That's not the way it was for me." Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:52.560
"That's overstated." If we're creating&nbsp;
a reaction, it's going to get in the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:58.600
of the conversation that we're having because a&nbsp;
book is a one-way conversation. So we're trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.600 --> 00:22:05.000
to anticipate what readers are wondering about&nbsp;
or objecting to or confused by or feel is off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:12.280
base and then address it right away in the book.
So the good news was that we had an example that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.280 --> 00:22:19.080
was generating a lot of reader reaction that&nbsp;
people were bringing their own experience to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:26.720
That's good. But the fact that then it tells the&nbsp;
story as if there's one way to handle it, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:34.200
this was a relatively mild version that didn't&nbsp;
have power differentials in the way that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:42.920
people have faced, meant that I suddenly, because&nbsp;
it was my example, was feeling defensive and like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:48.720
"Well, you're misunderstanding me." And so we&nbsp;
did an interim fix where we tried to clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:55.360
there was no HR, that this wasn't the only way to&nbsp;
handle it. The interim fix totally didn't work.&nbsp;

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:59.440
Tim Ferriss: So it wasn't clear in the&nbsp;
original edition that you did not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:04.920
HR or some intermediary to go to,&nbsp;
or it wasn't sufficiently clear?&nbsp;

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:08.160
Sheila Heen: We felt that it was clear. Yes, we&nbsp;
felt that it was clear. So that's the thing is we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:15.200
felt that it was incredibly clear and that other&nbsp;
people were misunderstanding us. And we had a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:19.800
list of all the things that were wrong with their&nbsp;
reactions and understanding. So we tried to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:23.187
clearer and clearer with the example —
Tim Ferriss: And it didn't work.&nbsp;

00:23:23.187 --> 00:23:26.440
Sheila Heen: It didn't work. It didn't&nbsp;
work at all. So what we needed to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:35.960
which was what we finally did was say, "It's not&nbsp;
about my experience, actually. It's about what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:49.000
readers are bringing to their own life experience&nbsp;
and the felt pain that they want help with. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:55.320
this example, the way we're telling it is not&nbsp;
meeting them where they're at and is a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.320 --> 00:24:01.640
version which is not universal." So eventually,&nbsp;
in the third edition, we took it out. We replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:06.360
it with a different example that got to the points&nbsp;
we were trying to make with it because we realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:13.120
that we're just creating more noise than help.
Tim Ferriss: Let me ask a question about that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:18.960
because the situation itself is&nbsp;
outside of my personal experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:24.850
but receiving feedback on books —
Sheila Heen: Yes, is your personal experience.&nbsp;

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:28.600
Tim Ferriss: — and revising books is in my&nbsp;
personal experience. So I'm very curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:37.400
did you receive strong positive feedback on that&nbsp;
particular example from other readers, or was it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:50.120
pretty uniformly negative or confusion? Or did&nbsp;
you also receive a equal amount or comparable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:56.640
amount of positive feedback from readers?
Sheila Heen: There were a couple of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:05.480
who came forward to say, "Oh, I actually really&nbsp;
loved that example. It immediately helped me see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:12.880
situations where, 'Oh, I feel more empowered.'&nbsp;
I can see what I would do differently." I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:21.120
of course, remember those really clearly because&nbsp;
I hung onto them so tightly. They were definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:30.840
way outweighed in this case. And you're raising&nbsp;
something that's super common, whether you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:37.560
a writer or just a human, which is, "You're&nbsp;
criticizing something, you hate this thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:43.800
but actually don't you understand that's the best&nbsp;
part of me? That's what everybody else loves."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:48.920
And so you get conflicting reader feedback.
Tim Ferriss: Could you say more about that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:53.600
The part that other people like about&nbsp;
you is that sort of contribution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:58.600
reclaiming of agency, is that what you mean?
Sheila Heen: No. That often the things that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:04.720
get feedback about from some people, our reaction&nbsp;
is like, "Okay, well, that's just contradictory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:09.387
because everybody else is telling me that that's&nbsp;
one of the things that they love about me."&nbsp;

00:26:09.387 --> 00:26:13.640
Tim Ferriss: I see.
Sheila Heen: So talk to me by the way about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:18.040
reader feedback that you get because I do think&nbsp;
it goes into different buckets. Some is just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:22.760
well, people have different preferences.&nbsp;
And so some people love this part, some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:29.240
people hate this part or wish it were different.
Tim Ferriss: I should say a few things. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:36.120
is that I try to, in the case of reader feedback,&nbsp;
which is different from proofreader feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:44.120
they're very different, because when I reach&nbsp;
out to proofreaders, I know the sample size.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:49.320
If I reach out to 10 proofreaders, I can&nbsp;
look at which responses are positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:56.520
negative, neutral, 10 out of 10. But when&nbsp;
you get negative feedback or criticism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.520 --> 00:27:02.880
there's a selection bias. If you are operating a&nbsp;
customer service hotline, chances are that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:07.240
who are thrilled with their experience are not&nbsp;
going to be the ones who call in or they're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:17.560
to be far fewer. So it's easy to sometimes&nbsp;
perceive that something is being uniformly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:23.240
across the board, criticized when in reality,&nbsp;
for instance, this has happened in the podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:27.280
where I've talked about certain things that I've&nbsp;
done or said that have come under fire, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:32.560
people come out of the woodwork to say, "Hey, we&nbsp;
are actually the silent majority who think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:37.480
great or totally fine, but we're not the ones who&nbsp;
are on Twitter yelling and screaming about it."&nbsp;

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:45.400
So I just want to point out that sort of selection&nbsp;
bias that I try to be aware of with. I pay more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:54.520
attention to proofreader feedback generally than&nbsp;
I do reader feedback. I pay attention to both. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.520 --> 00:28:01.840
instance, I was just giving someone this example&nbsp;
last night, The 4-Hour Workweek came out in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:08.640
and there was a quote at the head of a chapter&nbsp;
from Bill Cosby about breaking the rules that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:15.720
are set for you in an industry. And thank God&nbsp;
that a number of readers pointed out maybe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:20.320
should be taken out for the next printing. And&nbsp;
of course I agreed with that and took it out.&nbsp;

00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:24.040
Sheila Heen: Yeah, it quite differently.
Tim Ferriss: There's no upside to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:32.560
Only downside. It detracts from the point I'm&nbsp;
trying to make and it's a distraction, not an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:39.000
addition. There's really no upside for the reader&nbsp;
or for me in having that in. With proofreaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:47.080
and this will tie into the reader feedback, I&nbsp;
am sending, say, a given chapter or chapters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:52.880
to professional writers who are friends of mine&nbsp;
who ideally have different sensibilities also to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.880 --> 00:29:01.040
non-professional writers, so let's just call them&nbsp;
lay audience readers. And I'm generally asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:06.400
first and foremost, what do you find confusing?&nbsp;
Highlight anything that is unclear or confusing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:15.080
Second, highlight if I could only keep 20 percent&nbsp;
of this chapter, what I should absolutely keep.&nbsp;

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:24.000
And then if I had to cut 20 percent, have to,&nbsp;
which 20 percent would you cut? And my rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:32.520
with the proofreaders is if one person among my&nbsp;
small selection I've selected for good reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:39.280
if they love it, stays in. And take&nbsp;
something out usually requires a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:42.520
pattern. So if more than one person says,&nbsp;
"Take this out, it's slow, it's slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:46.440
it's confusing, it doesn't pull its own&nbsp;
weight," whatever that happens to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:51.000
then I take it out. Those are my three biggest.&nbsp;
I mean, if I added one more on, I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:58.760
please note in the margins or in the document&nbsp;
anywhere your mind starts to wander. So anytime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:04.200
you start thinking about your to-do list or your&nbsp;
email or something else, that's an indication that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:10.280
I'm not building sufficient traction and momentum&nbsp;
in the piece to keep you engaged and I have to fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.280 --> 00:30:14.160
that. Those are the things I focus on.
Sheila Heen: The connection is broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:18.800
between the voice of the book and the reader.
Tim Ferriss: Exactly. It's just not compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:25.680
enough. So I pay attention to those things. And&nbsp;
there are some revisions that I've made over time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:33.460
where again, there's not enough value and upside&nbsp;
to the reader or anyone else to justify a counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:39.920
argument on my part. For instance, in The 4-Hour&nbsp;
Workweek, again, I used the word "retarded" at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:46.920
point, and it's not going to personally offend&nbsp;
most people reading it, but it is outdated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:53.960
and it just wasn't necessary. It wasn't a critical&nbsp;
piece of a story. I could take it out. I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.960 --> 00:30:58.800
feel like I was compromising the integrity of&nbsp;
the writing by taking it out, so I took it out.&nbsp;

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:07.280
But in the case of your example, I would love to&nbsp;
know how you personally feel about it, about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:16.000
revision and the editing of that. Because for&nbsp;
instance, as I think about my preparation right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:21.640
now that I am undertaking for trips starting&nbsp;
next week where I'm going to be off the grid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:29.760
in some very remote, in some cases possibly&nbsp;
dangerous environments, I'm going with someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:35.000
who's former military. It's a long story that&nbsp;
I won't get into, but part of our conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:41.840
largely directed by him, has been what we&nbsp;
should wear, how we should pack, specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.840 --> 00:31:51.480
to minimize the likelihood of being a target, and&nbsp;
different types of behavior, different types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.480 --> 00:32:00.880
decision-making that are intended to minimize&nbsp;
the likelihood or reduce the likelihood of bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:12.320
things happening. From my perspective, it would&nbsp;
be irresponsible not to have that conversation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:19.000
And I'm grateful to have someone on my team who&nbsp;
has a lot of experience in these environments.&nbsp;

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:23.960
So this is just a way to say, I could say,&nbsp;
well, these bad things shouldn't happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:31.760
or these aggressors shouldn't have aggressive&nbsp;
behavior, but these things do happen. They will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:35.880
continue to happen. And so for me,&nbsp;
it's a question of risk mitigation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:41.600
and of assuming some degree of agency so that&nbsp;
we don't feel like a piece of driftwood being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:48.480
thrown around in an unpredictable sea entirely.&nbsp;
But how did you end up feeling about making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:52.920
revision? And by the way, I'm not saying you&nbsp;
should or shouldn't have made the revision, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.920 --> 00:33:01.240
just curious how it sits with you after the fact.
Sheila Heen: Mixed, of course. On the one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:08.280
relieved, we wrote every different possible&nbsp;
version to try to fix it as possible. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:15.080
just like, it's just not good. It's not serving&nbsp;
our purposes. And it might be too close to home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:26.040
too personal, so that my sense of it is muddied&nbsp;
and we really need to hear the impact that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:39.320
having, which is not helping our cause. And it is&nbsp;
such an important issue for so many people these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.320 --> 00:33:47.840
days, male and female, that it feels to me like a&nbsp;
conversation about all of the ways in which that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:56.960
feels painful and we can feel powerless and stuck,&nbsp;
et cetera. It feels like an important topic.&nbsp;

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:04.520
And it's a conversation that I have with people&nbsp;
a few times a year who come to me to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:11.480
I need help with this situation. So I'm mourning&nbsp;
a little bit that loss and I'm at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.480 --> 00:34:18.320
persuaded the use of that particular example in&nbsp;
this context was not helping. So let's just let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:27.080
it go and let me think about what I need to&nbsp;
learn about my own need to somehow have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:34.640
particularities of my experience feel heard by&nbsp;
readers rather than saying, the purpose is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.640 --> 00:34:40.800
bring forward your own. So that's the point&nbsp;
here. And Sheila, you're missing the point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:48.880
Can we circle to the proofreader feedback, right?
Tim Ferriss: Oh, sure. And also, I asked you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:54.160
bookmark something that we can come back to or&nbsp;
not, but you mentioned women overseas, maybe not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.160 --> 00:35:00.280
solely women, but who have contended with much&nbsp;
more extreme examples of your situation. So if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:05.240
we wanted to come back to that, happy to come back&nbsp;
to it. But yes, we can also talk about proofreader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.240 --> 00:35:11.440
feedback. So however you want to lead us.
Sheila Heen: Let's go to proofreader feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:21.200
because one of the things I really love about&nbsp;
how clear you are with your proofreaders is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:29.720
you are implicitly letting them know what kind&nbsp;
of feedback you're asking for. This is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:33.760
the typical mistakes that we make, which is that&nbsp;
we say, "Hey, could you take a look at this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:40.587
give me some feedback?" Do you have any feedback&nbsp;
for me is an incredibly hard question to answer.&nbsp;

00:35:40.587 --> 00:35:44.800
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, very ambiguous.
Sheila Heen: Very ambiguous. It's like, how honest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:50.400
should I be? Should I have prepared, et cetera.
Tim Ferriss: And if these people are your friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:50.400 --> 00:36:00.320
well, I choose my friends these days, I&nbsp;
would say with one critical leg of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:09.960
stool being their ability and eagerness to give&nbsp;
me what might be considered difficult feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:20.840
call my baby ugly if need be to save me some pain&nbsp;
later. But early on, and I'm sorry to interrupt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:29.640
but just to offer a contrast early on, I would ask&nbsp;
friends of mine to read and give me feedback in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:34.800
just as many words. And many of them assumed I was&nbsp;
looking for moral support. So they would be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:39.400
"Oh, I really liked this and this and this." And&nbsp;
they'd leave out the fact that they couldn't make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:46.840
any sense of three other things and really hated&nbsp;
two other things. But I was not surgical in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:52.800
request. So they gave me what they inferred I&nbsp;
wanted, which was a pat on the back and a pat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:58.867
on the head. And that's not actually what&nbsp;
I was asking for, but that was my fault.&nbsp;

00:36:58.867 --> 00:37:03.160
Sheila Heen: Yes. And I think that's a big&nbsp;
thing that I have learned from the feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:08.360
work. When you say "my fault," what do you mean?
Tim Ferriss: Well, I felt a little resistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:13.160
there. Yes, they could have, if&nbsp;
they had been more proactive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.160 --> 00:37:22.280
they could have asked me to refine or they could&nbsp;
have taken a role in teasing out a more precise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.280 --> 00:37:27.240
request. So I don't want to put it all on me,&nbsp;
but I also fumbled the ball. So they could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.240 --> 00:37:31.360
picked it up, but I also fumbled it a bit.
Sheila Heen: I think you're exactly right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.360 --> 00:37:40.200
which is to have a rich and meaningful feedback&nbsp;
conversation, there's responsibility on both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:47.840
sides. And you are pretty clear, and your&nbsp;
friends have learned, the ones who didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:55.280
learn didn't make the cut anymore, so they&nbsp;
have learned what you value in terms of pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.280 --> 00:37:58.360
candid coaching and evaluation.
Tim Ferriss: And actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.360 --> 00:37:59.667
may I add one more thing?
Sheila Heen: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:37:59.667 --> 00:38:06.120
Tim Ferriss: The people who are best at&nbsp;
giving feedback, they will sometimes ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:08.387
for further refinements in my request.
Sheila Heen: Yeah, clarification.&nbsp;

00:38:08.387 --> 00:38:12.400
Tim Ferriss: They'll be like, "Okay, well, when&nbsp;
you say this is what you're really looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:15.800
this or this," they will drill&nbsp;
down until they're like, "Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.800 --> 00:38:22.160
I can repeat back exactly what you're asking of&nbsp;
me." Great. Deadline, next Monday. Perfect. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:25.960
then they're on it, something like that.
Sheila Heen: And I think it takes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.960 --> 00:38:29.920
exchange. So you, I'm sure you have this&nbsp;
experience, which I also have, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:35.560
that other people hand me their manuscripts. This&nbsp;
was true two weeks ago and say, could you take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:41.280
look at this and give me some feedback? And I&nbsp;
have learned that I need to say more. Can you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:46.520
say more about what would be helpful to you at&nbsp;
this stage? And just to put a finer point on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:51.720
one of the things that has really helped me is to&nbsp;
understand that there are really three kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:59.000
feedback. We need all three over time to learn&nbsp;
and grow, but we might need different types at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:06.360
different times. And the first is appreciation.&nbsp;
And sometimes with a manuscript at the stage it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:13.200
at, what you need to know, is there anything&nbsp;
here of value? What do you like here? Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:21.720
I just need a little encouragement to keep going.
And I think the appreciation plays a huge role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:27.480
our willingness to stay engaged over time in the&nbsp;
relationship. If I feel like you really see me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:33.240
and get how hard I'm working or there are things&nbsp;
that you like and appreciate about me even in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:40.000
midst of us having some struggle. The second kind&nbsp;
is coaching. Coaching is anything designed to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:48.000
it be better, help me be better. You are being&nbsp;
really clear with your readers. Tell me what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:55.120
confusing. Coach me on where I'm not being clear.&nbsp;
And coaching course is the big engine for learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:59.600
and change and improvement. But you're also&nbsp;
actually asking for the third kind of feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:05.600
which is evaluation, like rate or rank it.&nbsp;
And what you're saying is there's a standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:12.800
for what stays and what goes. Tell me what&nbsp;
must stay like it well meets the standard for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:19.800
being essential. Tell me what doesn't make the&nbsp;
cut in your view. And that's actually helpful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.800 --> 00:40:28.800
to me. So you're actually asking primarily&nbsp;
for coaching and evaluation in your request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:36.707
and when people are that clear about what they're&nbsp;
looking for, it's so much easier to respond.&nbsp;

00:40:36.707 --> 00:40:42.360
Tim Ferriss: If I could just bounce off of that a&nbsp;
few things. First is that I will also frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:48.820
not ask someone to check all of those boxes.
Sheila Heen: Right. Often it's one thing.&nbsp;

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:51.360
Tim Ferriss: You're right. When I'm asking&nbsp;
professional writers especially they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:56.480
their own deadlines, they have their own work.&nbsp;
Reading my stuff is not their job, so I'm asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.480 --> 00:41:05.720
them for a favor. Very often I'll ask them to&nbsp;
just indicate the 20 percent that has to go or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:12.520
the 20 percent that has to stay or just indicate&nbsp;
what is unclear or slow where you start to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:12.520 --> 00:41:21.760
about other things. And I'll make up for the&nbsp;
lightness of that request by adding more people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:28.520
I'll just have more proofreaders instead of more&nbsp;
requests per proofreader. And I should also say, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:36.000
want to give credit where credit is due. So Steven&nbsp;
Pressfield, incredible writer in his own right and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:44.560
has written The War of Art and many other books,&nbsp;
talks about resistance. And he became an informal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.560 --> 00:41:51.480
coach/therapist when I was working on fiction for&nbsp;
the first time about, let's call it a year ago.&nbsp;

00:41:51.480 --> 00:42:02.840
And I sent him some very early drafts and asked&nbsp;
him for feedback being, I think in some respects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.840 --> 00:42:14.640
masochistic. I asked him to just vivisect my work.&nbsp;
And his response was basically, "I've read it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:20.440
I want to read more, just keep going." And&nbsp;
what I gleaned from that was that he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.440 --> 00:42:25.400
"That's great kid. I recognize that you want me&nbsp;
to tear this apart and that's not what you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:31.120
right now. So I'm actually going to give you the&nbsp;
appreciation, not the coaching and evaluation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:36.640
I'm going to give you the pat on the back and&nbsp;
the kick in the ass just to keep going because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:42.640
actually that's what you need right now." And&nbsp;
he was right. Of course, I'm speculating a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:48.240
about his inner experience, but that's I think the&nbsp;
intention behind the feedback that he gave me. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:56.160
how do people take this framework, the three kinds&nbsp;
of feedback and put it into practice, I suppose?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:56.720 --> 00:43:03.600
What are some ways that people might think about&nbsp;
carrying that into their day? They listen to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:08.200
they carry it into their day or their week.
Sheila Heen: Yeah. Well, one thing that helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.200 --> 00:43:18.520
is just to be clearer about what you're short on&nbsp;
right now or what you're asking someone for. Or if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:25.320
you're being asked to give feedback, to ask them&nbsp;
and maybe not trust their answer. What I hear him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:33.760
saying is, implicitly we're speculating, maybe, "I&nbsp;
think what you just need is the encouragement to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:39.960
keep going right now." He could also be thinking&nbsp;
in the early stages of any fiction, there are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.960 --> 00:43:45.320
hundred things wrong with it, but you don't figure&nbsp;
out what they are until you're further down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.320 --> 00:43:50.120
road and you are coming back and revising. So&nbsp;
you're just not at the stage that's a good match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:58.720
for specific coaching because you just need to&nbsp;
keep going. Once you see where this arc goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.720 --> 00:44:04.040
you'll back up and see and figure out what needs&nbsp;
to change, who knows? He's got some philosophy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:10.640
his own head. In terms of getting specific about&nbsp;
things you can do with this framework of types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.640 --> 00:44:16.840
of feedback, they have different purposes. Easy&nbsp;
way to remember them is ACE, A-C-E, appreciation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.840 --> 00:44:22.400
coaching and evaluation. We need all three.
Tim Ferriss: Could you just perhaps spend a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:28.440
little more time differentiating between coaching&nbsp;
and evaluation? Because I could see mixing those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:28.440 --> 00:44:32.960
up. I could see personally mixing those up.&nbsp;
I think of coaching as part of evaluation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:38.120
So how should we distinguish those two?
Sheila Heen: They often get tangled. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.120 --> 00:44:43.720
often I'm very clear that what I'm offering&nbsp;
you is coaching for reasons that have to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:43.720 --> 00:44:49.920
with your personality problems. You're hearing&nbsp;
it as judgment or evaluation. So there's often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:49.920 --> 00:44:55.960
a mismatch between what the giver thinks&nbsp;
they're doing and what the receiver hears.&nbsp;

00:44:55.960 --> 00:45:01.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it happens a lot.
Sheila Heen: And partly that reflects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:06.600
fact that packed into, implicit in any coaching,&nbsp;
is a little bit of evaluation. Because if I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:12.400
giving you a suggestion on how to do it better,&nbsp;
it suggests that you could be doing it better. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:18.280
so we hear that, which hits an identity thing.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. So is coaching the what and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:30.000
the how of improving, whereas the evaluation is&nbsp;
basically fail/pass, A+. It's basically giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.920
someone a value judgment on their work.&nbsp;
Good enough, not good enough. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:39.000
the coaching is here are the steps to take.
Sheila Heen: Yeah. So we have some set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:47.280
standards for whether this manuscript is ready to&nbsp;
go, is it ready to go out or not? What standards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:53.560
for performance expectations? In this role,&nbsp;
here's what we would expect from you. Grades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.560 --> 00:46:02.280
obviously. Here's what I'm looking for in terms&nbsp;
of the quality of your work. So yes, evaluation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:10.600
rating or ranking against some set of expectations&nbsp;
or standards how you measure up. So just asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:10.600 --> 00:46:18.320
"Am I on track for what you expected from me&nbsp;
in this new role?" So the question of "How is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:25.080
this going in our relationship?" is often,&nbsp;
"How are you feeling about whether this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.080 --> 00:46:31.960
still exceeding your expectations?" or starting&nbsp;
to feel wanting. That goes along with a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.960 --> 00:46:39.320
bunch of coaching potentially for what I wish you&nbsp;
would do. So the two are very closely related,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.320 --> 00:46:47.880
but we have the biggest emotional reaction to&nbsp;
the evaluation part because we hate being judged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:47.880 --> 00:46:55.387
It's hard. It's really hard to feel judged.&nbsp;
And so we're quick to hear it in anything.&nbsp;

00:46:55.387 --> 00:47:05.600
Tim Ferriss: I was looking at some of the prep&nbsp;
notes and one tactic, or maybe it's more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:13.600
practice that I'd love for you to describe, is&nbsp;
the phone a friend. Because this seems like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:24.640
low-risk, high-reward way to cultivate the ability&nbsp;
to look at things from multiple perspectives and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.640 --> 00:47:32.200
to receive feedback well. So the supportive&nbsp;
mirror, the honest mirror. Could you describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:32.200 --> 00:47:37.840
for folks what the phone a friend means here?
Sheila Heen: So part of the challenge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.840 --> 00:47:46.360
receiving feedback is, as I think I mentioned&nbsp;
a little while ago, when feedback is incoming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:46.920 --> 00:47:53.040
formal or informal, I'm scanning for what's wrong&nbsp;
with it. As human beings, we're very good at wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.040 --> 00:48:00.880
spotting. What they're saying isn't true. That's&nbsp;
not exactly how it happened or in the context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.880 --> 00:48:05.160
that wasn't what was going to help. Who is giving&nbsp;
it to me? There's all kinds of problems. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:09.320
like them. I don't trust them. I don't want&nbsp;
to be like them. They don't know what they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:09.320 --> 00:48:16.600
talking about. Why I suspect they're giving it&nbsp;
to me. They've got their own agenda for how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.600 --> 00:48:23.080
want me to change. When and where and how they&nbsp;
gave it to me was outrageous and ridiculous.&nbsp;

00:48:23.080 --> 00:48:28.760
So if I can come up with what's wrong with it,&nbsp;
then I can safely set it aside and relax and go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:34.000
on with my life. And the problem with that is that&nbsp;
you're always going to be able to find something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:40.880
wrong with the feedback that you get. And it could&nbsp;
be 90 percent wrong, but that last 10 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:47.800
might be just what I need to be thinking about in&nbsp;
order to change and grow. So for instance, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:47.800 --> 00:48:52.777
they understood happened and why I did it doesn't&nbsp;
make sense and their suggestion for what to do —&nbsp;

00:48:52.777 --> 00:48:58.160
It doesn't make sense, and their suggestion&nbsp;
for what to do instead wouldn't work. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:58.160 --> 00:49:01.480
maybe the 10 percent is that they're&nbsp;
highlighting that this is maybe a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.480 --> 00:49:05.120
problem than I thought it was. I have my own&nbsp;
diagnosis and I'll come up with my own solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:05.120 --> 00:49:10.040
but they're putting something on my plate&nbsp;
that maybe wasn't as visible to me before.&nbsp;

00:49:10.040 --> 00:49:16.040
Part of the skill of becoming a better receiver&nbsp;
is not letting my triggered reaction and my wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:16.040 --> 00:49:22.840
spotting be the end of the story to throw it all&nbsp;
out, but instead to first work to understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.840 --> 00:49:27.360
feedback. Then I can decide and sort what is&nbsp;
right about it and what's wrong with it. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:32.320
that's really hard for me to do by myself for&nbsp;
a bunch of reasons in terms of the challenge to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:36.560
see what the giver means and the challenge&nbsp;
to see myself accurately and all of that.&nbsp;

00:49:36.560 --> 00:49:41.560
And so I can phone a friend to help. By the way,&nbsp;
I'm going to phone a friend anyway. When I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:41.560 --> 00:49:48.320
upsetting feedback, I'm going to go complain to&nbsp;
somebody else about it. Right? Over a glass of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:53.240
wine. And we're going to make a big list about&nbsp;
what's wrong with this other person and all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:53.240 --> 00:50:01.800
ways in which they're wrong. And so what I can do&nbsp;
is I can reach out to somebody I trust, which I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:07.400
going to do anyway and I'm implicitly asking&nbsp;
them to be what we call a supportive mirror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:07.400 --> 00:50:12.720
to join me in seeing what's wrong with it.
Because if I'm really devastated by it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:19.160
I'm not in a place where I can learn anyway. This&nbsp;
one thing and this one mistake is the whole story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.160 --> 00:50:23.880
about me. And I'm so knocked off balance that —
Tim Ferriss: Or if you're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.880 --> 00:50:28.680
annoyed enough that you're spun up.
Sheila Heen: Spun up, and rejecting it, and pissed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.680 --> 00:50:36.480
off. Right? Absolutely. This is just more evidence&nbsp;
of what's wrong with them. I'm not in a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.480 --> 00:50:44.640
place and I need somebody else to join me in just&nbsp;
getting myself back to a place where I can maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.640 --> 00:50:51.640
hear what might be right about it. Asking a friend&nbsp;
two questions. Ask you two questions and help you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.640 --> 00:50:55.760
process in two directions. One is what we're doing&nbsp;
naturally, which is be as supportive mirror. Join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.760 --> 00:50:59.600
me in asking what's wrong with this feedback.
And then when I'm ready — usually, we quit there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:07.680
and go home because now we feel better. In the&nbsp;
meantime, our friend is thinking it's not totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:13.520
off base. And so we need to give them permission&nbsp;
to then when we're ready to shift and be what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:13.520 --> 00:51:24.440
we call an honest mirror and to help us see what&nbsp;
might be right about it. What might be legitimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:33.400
and maybe hard for me to see when we have a visual&nbsp;
for the supportive mirror, it's like a gold gilded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.400 --> 00:51:41.440
frame. Show me at my best in flattering light.&nbsp;
They're wrong. I'm okay. I'm fabulous. And we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:45.080
need that. It's important not to skip it.
Tim Ferriss: And what might be right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.080 --> 00:51:48.880
about it is more like black mirror.
Sheila Heen: It's like a black mirror.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:48.880 --> 00:51:54.000
The mirror that we often use is actually —&nbsp;
you know that hand mirror that you find in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:57.760
barbershops or hair salons?
Tim Ferriss: Sure.&nbsp;

00:51:57.760 --> 00:52:05.640
Sheila Heen: And the reason — also black mirror.&nbsp;
The reason we use that shape of mirror is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.640 --> 00:52:12.787
when the barber or the hairstylist shows it to&nbsp;
you, they're showing you the back of your head.&nbsp;

00:52:12.787 --> 00:52:16.440
Tim Ferriss: Oh, right. When they walk behind you.
Sheila Heen: They spin you around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:19.760
so that you can look in the mirror and see, you&nbsp;
can appreciate what an amazing job they did. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:19.760 --> 00:52:25.440
they're actually handing it to you to help you see&nbsp;
something that you can't see by yourself. Being an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:25.440 --> 00:52:30.920
honest mirror is asking a friend to help you see&nbsp;
what might be right about this feedback. What do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:30.920 --> 00:52:40.280
you think I should probably hear embedded in it?
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so I definitely encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.280 --> 00:52:46.720
people to do this. I mean, I naturally do&nbsp;
this with a number of my close friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:48.080 --> 00:52:53.227
is ask them for that barber spin&nbsp;
around, look at the back of the head.&nbsp;

00:52:53.227 --> 00:52:57.160
Sheila Heen: Right. Right. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Here's a question that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:57.160 --> 00:53:04.720
is not directly related, but it's certainly&nbsp;
thematically aligned with what we're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.720 --> 00:53:09.120
about in this conversation. Also, what&nbsp;
we talked about in our last conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:18.160
things have changed since we last spoke. I am back&nbsp;
in the singles arena and one thing that has struck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:29.080
me is almost no number of dinners every one or two&nbsp;
weeks, where everyone's on best behavior allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.080 --> 00:53:34.800
you to see what people are like in difficult&nbsp;
conversations. Unless it's heavily engineered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:34.800 --> 00:53:43.200
it's unlikely to come up organically. Unless, who&nbsp;
knows, the waiter spills tomato juice on her dress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:48.720
and she freaks out and smashes a glass on the&nbsp;
floor and starts yelling at people. Okay, fine.&nbsp;

00:53:48.720 --> 00:53:50.840
Sheila Heen: You're like,&nbsp;
"Well, thanks for the clarity."&nbsp;

00:53:50.840 --> 00:53:57.840
Tim Ferriss: But I haven't yet architected&nbsp;
something like that. But I am curious to know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:57.840 --> 00:54:00.160
and I'll give a counter example&nbsp;
actually. I'm not going to name names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:00.160 --> 00:54:08.880
but I was at the dinner with someone I know who,&nbsp;
last I saw him, was actually, he was single,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:12.880
then decided he wanted to find a partner and&nbsp;
basically made a commitment to every — he had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.880 --> 00:54:16.520
one-pager. He would show each person on a date&nbsp;
and he'd be like, "What do you think of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:18.800
one-pager? This is what I'm looking for."
Sheila Heen: Oh, my gosh. What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

