gap when that person is the baseline. I'm curious&nbsp;
about your reactions to that. Another question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:46.680 --> 01:11:52.880
who am I in this relationship as it's starting&nbsp;
to develop? Do I like the way that I am in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.880 --> 01:11:56.227
relationship and the way I'm showing up.
Tim Ferriss: That's a big one for me.&nbsp;

01:11:56.227 --> 01:11:59.200
Sheila Heen: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Right. Does this person help me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:59.200 --> 01:12:06.507
be a better version of myself, or is this person&nbsp;
sort of summoning the demons of my lesser self?&nbsp;

01:12:06.507 --> 01:12:09.067
Sheila Heen: Yeah, exactly.
Tim Ferriss: That's a real thing.&nbsp;

01:12:09.067 --> 01:12:12.880
Sheila Heen: It's a real thing. And I'll name&nbsp;
the third thing, and then we'll come back to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.880 --> 01:12:20.680
thing, which is just, okay, what's the you plus&nbsp;
me combination? How authentic and honest are we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:20.680 --> 01:12:24.240
being with each other? Do I know what to expect?&nbsp;
Do I know how you feel? Do you know how I feel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:24.240 --> 01:12:28.880
et cetera? And how do we handle the inevitable&nbsp;
friction that we're going to have? Because in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:28.880 --> 01:12:33.800
any combination of two people, you've got&nbsp;
lots of things that are complementary and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:33.800 --> 01:12:39.080
some things that are going to have some friction&nbsp;
just because you have really different preferences&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:39.080 --> 01:12:46.560
and habits and interests and whatever.
In how we handle that, does it bring out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:46.560 --> 01:12:51.640
the better part of me or the worst part of me&nbsp;
and vice versa? And does it feel fair also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:51.640 --> 01:12:57.200
or does it feel like one of us is always the one&nbsp;
who has to apologize or to change? And so say a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:57.200 --> 01:13:02.347
little bit more about bringing out the better&nbsp;
parts of you and the worst parts of you.&nbsp;

01:13:02.347 --> 01:13:11.560
Tim Ferriss: Well, let me preface that by saying I&nbsp;
did a Q&amp;A with some of my audience yesterday. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.560 --> 01:13:18.960
if my talking about relationship stuff on the&nbsp;
podcast could be a Rorschach test, holy shit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:18.960 --> 01:13:24.160
do I get a lot of stuff projected on me. There&nbsp;
were so many questions like, "Tim, why can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:24.160 --> 01:13:30.320
you hold onto a partner?" Da, da, da, da, da.&nbsp;
"What happened? Why can't you learn the lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:30.320 --> 01:13:36.480
about this, this, and this?" All of these things.
I would just say just briefly that I actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.480 --> 01:13:43.440
if you were to talk to my last few exes, who I'm&nbsp;
still on good terms with, they would, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:43.440 --> 01:13:49.080
all say that I'm a really good boyfriend. I'm&nbsp;
not perfect, but I talk about this in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:49.080 --> 01:13:56.240
way that I might, if I had an expert on language&nbsp;
learning on, I would ask them how I could improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:56.240 --> 01:14:00.920
in language learning, even though I think I'm&nbsp;
pretty good at it and have a demonstrated track&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:00.920 --> 01:14:06.280
record. I would still want to be better because I&nbsp;
know there's room for improvement. I just want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:06.280 --> 01:14:13.880
set the table a little bit so that I maybe stem&nbsp;
the flow of, "Tim, why can't you figure anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.880 --> 01:14:20.720
out in relationship" type questions in my open&nbsp;
Q&amp;A sessions that I do every once in a while.&nbsp;

01:14:20.720 --> 01:14:23.040
But to come back to —
Sheila Heen: You are naming that that's really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.040 --> 01:14:31.160
why can't I figure out? Because it's easier for&nbsp;
me to see it you not figuring something out. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:31.160 --> 01:14:38.440
partly my seeing that is motivated by in my own&nbsp;
life. Why can't I figure out what's going wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.440 --> 01:14:43.200
in my own relationship or where I'm stuck? Or it&nbsp;
might be I was stuck for a long time and gosh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:43.200 --> 01:14:47.120
I want to help you. But it's hard for us to see.
Tim Ferriss: A lot of it's people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.120 --> 01:14:51.000
vomiting their own stuff.
Sheila Heen: Totally. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:53.760 --> 01:14:56.680
totally a Rorschach test.
Tim Ferriss: Yes, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:56.680 --> 01:15:07.400
and yes. I would say that more than almost any&nbsp;
of the questions or criteria you mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:07.400 --> 01:15:15.600
the one that gives me a clear signal is, "Do I&nbsp;
like who I am around this person?" That's it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:15.600 --> 01:15:22.600
some matches work better than others, obviously.&nbsp;
Some combinations are better than others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:22.600 --> 01:15:26.600
and sometimes there are massive trade-offs.&nbsp;
Maybe you have excellent chemistry with someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:26.600 --> 01:15:37.360
but it's also so combustible and the communication&nbsp;
is probably sort of handicapped in some way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:37.360 --> 01:15:43.480
that's going to be disastrous ultimately. Right?
The read that I ask though, because it's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:43.480 --> 01:15:49.800
that I can confirm myself. If I ask a question&nbsp;
about someone else or I make an assumption, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:49.800 --> 01:15:56.160
instance, that based on how they're interacting&nbsp;
in a first or second date, I think they're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:56.160 --> 01:16:00.400
to be really bad at deescalating conflict. They&nbsp;
seem to step on top of — if they seem to interrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:00.400 --> 01:16:06.720
and step on top of me a lot, I might say, "It&nbsp;
seems like X." And they say, "Oh, wow. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.720 --> 01:16:12.240
you said I hated or loved X." And I'd say, "Well,&nbsp;
actually, no. That's a very exaggerated version&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:12.240 --> 01:16:17.400
of what I just said, but those are not my words."
And getting this taste test, I might infer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:17.400 --> 01:16:22.360
they would be bad at deescalating conflict, but I&nbsp;
can't really know that for sure based on a first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.360 --> 01:16:28.360
date. Maybe they're just nervous. Maybe they had&nbsp;
too much coffee. Who knows? I can assess though&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:28.360 --> 01:16:34.720
how I feel, hopefully with higher fidelity.&nbsp;
I pay attention to that because I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.720 --> 01:16:46.960
a more reliable, knowable, defensible feeling or&nbsp;
assessment. I do pay a lot of attention to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:46.960 --> 01:16:54.280
Right? Is someone dredging up all the stuff&nbsp;
that I haven't had to deal with for a while?&nbsp;

01:16:54.280 --> 01:16:58.800
Now, it's not to say I can easily point the&nbsp;
causality. Maybe there are other things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:58.800 --> 01:17:04.120
are happening that day, or that week, or that&nbsp;
month that have set the conditions. Or, do I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:04.120 --> 01:17:11.640
feel at ease? Are we laughing easily together,&nbsp;
et cetera? Am I feeling good about who I am with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:11.640 --> 01:17:22.680
this person, is very much at the top of my list.
Sheila Heen: Yeah, and part of what I love about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:31.600
that is that you're paying attention to what is&nbsp;
being called forward. Years ago, I was in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:31.600 --> 01:17:40.160
of those on again, off again, break up, get back&nbsp;
together, terrible relationships that I couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:40.160 --> 01:17:53.440
quite see my way out of at the time, and my mom,&nbsp;
who doesn't tend to say much explicitly, my mom&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:53.440 --> 01:18:01.080
gave lots of generalized coaching and instruction&nbsp;
to us as kids, but she wouldn't tend to comment on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:01.080 --> 01:18:06.520
something specific happening in the moment. She'd&nbsp;
give general advice. It was up to you to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:06.520 --> 01:18:09.227
out, "Oh, this is one of those things."
Tim Ferriss: Where it applied?&nbsp;

01:18:09.227 --> 01:18:12.840
Sheila Heen: Yeah, one of those things. She'd&nbsp;
kind of leave you alone in the middle of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:12.840 --> 01:18:18.120
But in the middle of it, she said to me one&nbsp;
night, "At this stage, I don't know that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:18.120 --> 01:18:26.920
supposed to be this hard." Somehow that was&nbsp;
the light bulb that was like, "Yeah. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.920 --> 01:18:35.000
it's not supposed to be this hard, this&nbsp;
early." I was so appreciative at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:43.880
and it was just an observation, so I'll also then&nbsp;
observe that focusing on, "Gosh, where is this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:43.880 --> 01:18:50.600
I'm really enjoying the conversation. This is&nbsp;
really bringing forward my best self," then sits&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:50.600 --> 01:18:57.000
in tension with, and what happens when we get&nbsp;
in conflict because then it doesn't test that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:57.000 --> 01:19:02.080
sometimes. So what's your experience with that?
Tim Ferriss: Totally. Well, I would say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.080 --> 01:19:08.320
there's someone trying to make you&nbsp;
feel good. It's not that hard if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:08.320 --> 01:19:13.320
just go with the heteronormative thing for&nbsp;
a second. If a woman laughs at your jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:13.320 --> 01:19:23.200
you feel great. So it's not hard for a woman&nbsp;
to realize that and play that hand very well.&nbsp;

01:19:23.200 --> 01:19:29.680
There's someone trying to make you feel good,&nbsp;
and then there's just how you feel from a state&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:29.680 --> 01:19:39.400
perspective being around them, being exposed&nbsp;
to them. And so I would say it doesn't show you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:39.400 --> 01:19:46.000
the play by play of how something will unfold&nbsp;
in conflict. And if someone is over-the-top&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.000 --> 01:19:52.840
cheerful all the time, that is definitely a&nbsp;
yellow flag for me because that isn't reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.440 --> 01:20:01.480
And when the pendulum swings really far in that&nbsp;
towards the mask of everything is always great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:01.480 --> 01:20:08.480
in my experience, tends to swing very hard back&nbsp;
in the other direction equally into the darkness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:08.480 --> 01:20:20.120
so I'm cautious around that. But I would say that&nbsp;
if someone can make me feel physiologically calm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:20.120 --> 01:20:24.680
and relaxed and that is a byproduct of their&nbsp;
personality, not necessarily the words they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:24.680 --> 01:20:35.440
say or how they laugh at my jokes, that, that&nbsp;
often I think is a foreshadowing of having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:35.440 --> 01:20:37.947
more easeful time in conflict.
Sheila Heen: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:20:37.947 --> 01:20:46.120
Tim Ferriss: If I am less activated by the&nbsp;
combination of our two personalities in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.120 --> 01:20:51.720
when things are heightened, I will probably&nbsp;
be more heightened, but maybe not DEFCON 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:51.720 --> 01:20:58.600
in which case I will just be able to speak,&nbsp;
listen, pause, suggest we table things for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:58.600 --> 01:21:06.240
while, and come back to them when we're a little&nbsp;
deescalated more effectively. At least that's how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:06.240 --> 01:21:11.560
suppose I'm thinking about it now&nbsp;
that I have to put words to it.&nbsp;

01:21:11.560 --> 01:21:18.640
Sheila Heen: And you're reminding me of something&nbsp;
that we were talking about last time and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.640 --> 01:21:28.480
you've mentioned today as well, which is working&nbsp;
something through to resolution. And I'm curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:28.480 --> 01:21:35.080
about what resolution feels like or looks like.&nbsp;
In other words, I think in lots of interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:35.080 --> 01:21:45.600
in relationship, someone is saying, "Hey, you&nbsp;
did that thing again. And by the way, I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.600 --> 01:21:54.400
want to note I'm annoyed right now because," fill&nbsp;
in the blank, and noting it helps me actually say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:54.400 --> 01:21:59.960
"And we don't have to talk about it now, but I&nbsp;
just needed to name it so that I don't instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.960 --> 01:22:06.760
act passive aggressively annoyed about it and&nbsp;
pretend I'm not." And then can we live in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:06.760 --> 01:22:11.800
space? Can we hold that space and we don't have&nbsp;
to totally unpack it right this very minute?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:11.800 --> 01:22:18.840
And sometimes that's enough. It's a chronic&nbsp;
thing between us and I'm just noting it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:18.840 --> 01:22:26.080
can we live in that space, both of us? Do we feel&nbsp;
equally comfortable or does one of us say, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:26.080 --> 01:22:31.120
now that you've said you're annoyed, we've got&nbsp;
to stop everything and spend a couple of hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:31.120 --> 01:22:35.507
unpacking it." And what's our preference there?
Tim Ferriss: That's my nightmare.&nbsp;

01:22:35.507 --> 01:22:36.520
Sheila Heen: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:36.520 --> 01:22:42.520
I kind of feel like a lot of processing, just&nbsp;
like a lot of presentations, fail from too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:42.520 --> 01:22:50.360
information, not too little information in the&nbsp;
sense that what does resolution look like to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:50.360 --> 01:22:58.040
if that's the question? I would answer&nbsp;
that I think the simplest way to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:58.040 --> 01:23:01.480
resolution is to have some agreement of&nbsp;
what that looks like in a shared framework,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:02.640 --> 01:23:06.600
something out of one of your books or&nbsp;
something from Getting the Love You Want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:06.600 --> 01:23:17.240
where there are actual triage tools that have&nbsp;
determined endpoints. Whether the endpoint is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:17.240 --> 01:23:22.480
"I just want to say this right now, we don't need&nbsp;
to address it. I'm not asking for processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:23.200 --> 01:23:29.240
but could we schedule a time to talk about it?"&nbsp;
Great, episode complete. I'm not saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:29.240 --> 01:23:39.280
as the one and only tool. The tool could be some&nbsp;
version of Nonviolent Communication where when X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:39.280 --> 01:23:48.280
Y, and Z happened, I felt this. The story I make&nbsp;
around that is this, could I ask you blank? Having&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:48.280 --> 01:23:58.400
a clear request and agreement and then episode&nbsp;
complete. So I think it's just having a mutual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:58.400 --> 01:24:05.160
agreement as to what those exchanges look like.
And I mean, a question that I want to ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:05.160 --> 01:24:09.360
because this came up in a number of places&nbsp;
in our last conversation, but we didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:09.360 --> 01:24:15.680
dig into this one piece in particular,&nbsp;
it's related to a line that I'll read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:15.680 --> 01:24:20.520
and there are a few different versions of this,&nbsp;
but "given Gottman's research, about two-thirds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:20.520 --> 01:24:24.840
of our conflicts and long-term relationships&nbsp;
are not really resolvable. The task isn't to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:24.840 --> 01:24:28.560
resolve them once and for all. It's to figure&nbsp;
out a way to handle them or navigate them and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:28.560 --> 01:24:36.240
manage them together that feels okay and good."
To zoom out, I do want to ask you about that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:36.240 --> 01:24:43.160
specifically is the only way to figure that out&nbsp;
to basically be with someone for six months or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:43.160 --> 01:24:50.320
a year. But broadly speaking, I'm wondering what&nbsp;
you think of the Gottman Institute. They get cited&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:50.320 --> 01:24:56.560
all the time because I'm sure people have heard,&nbsp;
and I'm going to get the specifics wrong here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:56.560 --> 01:25:03.360
but that the founders of the Gottman Institute&nbsp;
could look at five minutes of video footage of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:03.360 --> 01:25:07.480
couple, and with 95 percent plus accuracy,&nbsp;
predict who would be divorced within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:07.480 --> 01:25:11.600
next three years or whatever it might be.&nbsp;
People have heard these anecdotes. What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:11.600 --> 01:25:16.840
the Gottman Institute and what do you think the&nbsp;
strengths and weaknesses are to the extent that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:16.840 --> 01:25:20.800
you've been able to glean either of those things?
Sheila Heen: Yeah. Well, so I think that the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:20.800 --> 01:25:30.160
that they're doing is fascinating and additive and&nbsp;
there just aren't, particularly when they started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:30.160 --> 01:25:39.680
there weren't people really studying marriage&nbsp;
and long-term relationships, good, bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:39.680 --> 01:25:45.080
and everything in-between. So there might be&nbsp;
talk about why did things break down but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:45.080 --> 01:25:52.320
weren't also looking at what was working for other&nbsp;
couples. And from my point of view, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:52.320 --> 01:25:56.680
they're focused on what's most important,&nbsp;
which is that they are inviting couples in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:56.680 --> 01:26:01.000
they're inviting them to talk about a topic that&nbsp;
is stressful. So they're inviting them to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:01.000 --> 01:26:07.600
difficult conversation and they're hooking them&nbsp;
up to heart monitors and all of that. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:07.600 --> 01:26:15.680
they're recording all that data and then they're&nbsp;
following the couple and noticing what correlates&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.680 --> 01:26:24.920
with either self-reporting being happily married&nbsp;
and doing that over time. Because sometimes people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:24.920 --> 01:26:28.680
will self-report being happily married and then&nbsp;
they divorce and then they're going back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:28.680 --> 01:26:36.640
saying, "Well, actually I wasn't happily married,"&nbsp;
which may be revisionist memory, but also maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.640 --> 01:26:43.200
it was good enough for me to be happy, but then we&nbsp;
weren't talking about what we needed to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.200 --> 01:26:46.600
because there's a portion of those that are —
Tim Ferriss: The good was good enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:46.600 --> 01:26:48.880
but the bad wasn't being addressed.
Sheila Heen: Right, exactly. The bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:48.880 --> 01:26:52.640
wasn't being addressed. So it felt like, "Well, we&nbsp;
never argued about anything." Well, it's because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:52.640 --> 01:26:59.480
one of us wasn't speaking up to say the things&nbsp;
that were bothering us because it was good enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:59.480 --> 01:27:07.880
So I think that the work that they're doing is&nbsp;
incredibly valuable and it's very descriptive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:07.880 --> 01:27:16.960
meaning it's identifying what are the variables.&nbsp;
So eye-rolling is one of the things that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:16.960 --> 01:27:25.040
most closely correlated with the relationship&nbsp;
fraying and divorce, partly because eye-rolling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:25.040 --> 01:27:30.320
is just an external indicator of internal,&nbsp;
"Here we go again. I'm not really listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:30.320 --> 01:27:33.920
anymore." What they code as contempt.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah, very — I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:33.920 --> 01:27:38.227
not aware of a single culture where&nbsp;
eye-rolling has a positive indication.&nbsp;

01:27:38.227 --> 01:27:41.880
Sheila Heen: Right, exactly. Exactly. So what&nbsp;
they're doing is that they're identifying and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:41.880 --> 01:27:53.520
describing what correlates with a relationship&nbsp;
ending or seeming to have staying power. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:53.520 --> 01:27:58.920
I love about that is that it lines up so&nbsp;
nicely with the prescriptive work that we do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:58.920 --> 01:28:04.000
the diagnostic and prescriptive work that we do&nbsp;
to take a look at, well, what's really going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:04.000 --> 01:28:13.400
that's getting us stuck? Why am I rolling my eyes?&nbsp;
Because I feel frustrated, unheard, we're falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:13.400 --> 01:28:18.680
back into our usual pattern where I don't expect&nbsp;
there to be a resolution, and I'm exhausted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:18.680 --> 01:28:26.080
that and I don't know how to get out of it. So it&nbsp;
lines up nicely with our observations about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:26.080 --> 01:28:35.040
gets people stuck and also then what helps. But&nbsp;
it gives us insight through another lens about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:35.040 --> 01:28:45.480
longer term impact of that. The fact that about&nbsp;
a third of the conflicts tend to be temporal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:45.480 --> 01:28:51.200
you can say that they're resolvable or just we&nbsp;
move on because we had to decide where to live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.200 --> 01:28:55.760
and we decided, and now we're —
Tim Ferriss: Transient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:55.760 --> 01:28:58.040
They have an expiration date.
Sheila Heen: We're not arguing about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:58.040 --> 01:29:03.240
anymore. We needed to figure out how to parent&nbsp;
young children. We no longer have young children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:03.240 --> 01:29:11.000
Now we'll just have conflicts about how to parent&nbsp;
older children. So two thirds are just reflective&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:11.000 --> 01:29:18.680
of the particularities of you plus me and where&nbsp;
we're going to have some rub. And the question of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:18.680 --> 01:29:26.960
are we able to notice that, note it and hold it&nbsp;
with some humor and affection for each other in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:26.960 --> 01:29:34.720
the midst of it, even as we're honest and direct&nbsp;
about you doing that thing again. And do we feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:34.720 --> 01:29:42.920
like, I feel seen and heard, and it's a relatively&nbsp;
balanced sense of sometimes you get your way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:42.920 --> 01:29:50.320
sometimes I get my way, and we're slowly, it's&nbsp;
getting to be a less loaded topic rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:50.320 --> 01:29:58.227
a more loaded topic. What's your experience&nbsp;
with the loaded topics and eye-rolling, maybe?&nbsp;

01:29:58.227 --> 01:30:01.240
Tim Ferriss: I have very low tolerance&nbsp;
for eye-rolling. I just don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:01.240 --> 01:30:06.120
time for people who behave that way. So that's a&nbsp;
one-strike-you're-out kind of situation. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:06.120 --> 01:30:10.480
too many amazing people in the world, and there&nbsp;
are too many people who don't do that also. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:10.480 --> 01:30:16.320
are plenty of people who don't engage with that.
Let me preface, I'm going to ask a clarifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:16.320 --> 01:30:22.000
question about your question, but I will&nbsp;
say that I was thinking about it a bit as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:22.000 --> 01:30:28.440
you were discussing the Gottman Institute that&nbsp;
I don't say this much, but I've probably said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:28.440 --> 01:30:34.080
it a half a dozen times, observing couples&nbsp;
at dinners or at weddings where I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:34.080 --> 01:30:38.622
"They will be divorced within three&nbsp;
years." 100 percent accurate thus far.&nbsp;

01:30:38.622 --> 01:30:40.520
Sheila Heen: Yes. Yes. Yes.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:40.520 --> 01:30:41.120
I don't think it takes —
Sheila Heen: You should&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:41.120 --> 01:30:46.360
just be deputized to let people know.
Tim Ferriss: Well, I don't think it — honorary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:46.360 --> 01:30:48.663
degree from the Gottman Institute.
Sheila Heen: That's right.&nbsp;

01:30:48.663 --> 01:30:53.160
Tim Ferriss: But I honestly don't think it&nbsp;
takes Poirot or Sherlock Holmes to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:53.160 --> 01:30:59.800
it out. I mean, to me, a lot of it seems pretty&nbsp;
obvious, but the eye-rolling, making jokes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:59.800 --> 01:31:04.560
are at the expense of the other person where you&nbsp;
observe one person laughing who made the joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:04.560 --> 01:31:10.440
the other person clearly not laughing. Any&nbsp;
type of emasculating, which can go both ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:10.440 --> 01:31:19.800
but any type of cutting down in a group&nbsp;
environment seems so obviously a recipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:19.800 --> 01:31:26.400
for disaster. But when you say the loaded topics&nbsp;
or how I relate to that, could you phrase that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:26.400 --> 01:31:31.840
different way? What do you mean by that?
Sheila Heen: The topics that are chronic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:31.840 --> 01:31:40.000
irritations between us or differences, and then we&nbsp;
make meaning out of that. The fact that you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:40.000 --> 01:31:45.800
this drives me crazy and yet you still continue&nbsp;
to do it, or this is something we disagree&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:45.800 --> 01:31:54.200
about and it's still not feeling settled or good.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I suppose there are, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:54.200 --> 01:32:04.120
different species of chronic unresolved or&nbsp;
maybe unresolvable friction. I would say that I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:04.120 --> 01:32:09.760
certainly, in my last handful of relationships,&nbsp;
and it's not like I've had a ton. I mean, I've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:09.760 --> 01:32:17.920
a few long relationships. We've come to a truce,&nbsp;
I would say, on a lot of those things if we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:17.920 --> 01:32:23.760
laugh at them together, and sometimes they drive&nbsp;
one person or the other crazy. But there are these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:23.760 --> 01:32:33.920
manageable differences that can be more often than&nbsp;
not laughed about. I would say for me, as someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:33.920 --> 01:32:42.200
who has always battled chronic fatigue, and I do&nbsp;
think that could be explained partly by multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:42.200 --> 01:32:51.040
documented infections with Lyme disease, but&nbsp;
there are, I'm sure other contributing factors.&nbsp;

01:32:51.040 --> 01:32:57.840
The problem for me is not generally the problem,&nbsp;
unless somebody really betrays trust or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:57.840 --> 01:33:05.520
like that and does something that from the outside&nbsp;
would be considered terrible, clearly terrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:05.520 --> 01:33:08.120
which I had some degree of, what's the right&nbsp;
word? I would put up with more of that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:08.120 --> 01:33:14.280
I was very young. Like a lot of people, the&nbsp;
exciting person was worth all sorts of drama.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:14.280 --> 01:33:19.800
I'm just no longer at that place in my life.&nbsp;
So I don't deal with those types of situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:19.800 --> 01:33:27.640
really. But I would say the problem for me is&nbsp;
frequently not the problem itself, the situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:27.640 --> 01:33:33.960
that is under discussion. It's the method of&nbsp;
discussion and the duration of discussion.&nbsp;

01:33:33.960 --> 01:33:41.920
So for me, if constant processing becomes&nbsp;
a defining feature of the relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:41.920 --> 01:33:46.960
that is a meta issue for me that is very&nbsp;
difficult to metabolize, which doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:46.960 --> 01:33:51.240
mean I don't talk about problems. It's just&nbsp;
we should have a toolkit that allows us to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:51.240 --> 01:34:01.480
and also a degree of independent resilience and&nbsp;
interpersonal resilience, whereby if someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:01.480 --> 01:34:04.200
dropped a piece of gum on the floor and they don't&nbsp;
do it all the time, just pick it up and throw it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:04.200 --> 01:34:06.987
out. It's not a big deal. This doesn't require —
Sheila Heen: Accommodate the other person.&nbsp;

01:34:06.987 --> 01:34:16.600
Tim Ferriss: Does not require a conversation.&nbsp;
So I would say that for me, most of the issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:16.600 --> 01:34:22.440
at least those that are coming to mind right&nbsp;
now are not big in and of themselves. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:22.440 --> 01:34:28.200
the discussion ends up feeling like it's just&nbsp;
going in circles upon circles upon circles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:28.200 --> 01:34:29.787
then I do struggle with it.
Sheila Heen: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:34:29.787 --> 01:34:34.760
Tim Ferriss: That I struggle with, which is&nbsp;
certainly not unique to me. Every couple I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:34.760 --> 01:34:44.080
has some version of this, but certainly my hope is&nbsp;
that with someone I'm with long-term, the majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:44.080 --> 01:34:49.920
of time it does not feel like heavy lifting. Your&nbsp;
mom said to you, "At this point, I'm not sure it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:49.920 --> 01:34:53.507
should be this hard," something like that.
Sheila Heen: Yes.&nbsp;

01:34:53.507 --> 01:34:58.880
Tim Ferriss: And the couples I've talked to&nbsp;
who I really admire, who have been together 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:58.880 --> 01:35:07.040
20 plus years, generally all come back with some&nbsp;
version of that. It shouldn't be hard all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:07.040 --> 01:35:13.600
time. However, I find them all very difficult&nbsp;
to model because almost without exception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:13.600 --> 01:35:19.360
they met when they were very young.&nbsp;
Neither person had anything. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:19.360 --> 01:35:28.720
young and stupid and loving life. And they met&nbsp;
through complete luck of the draw circumstance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.720 --> 01:35:34.400
it would seem. Oftentimes very early, and&nbsp;
sometimes it's like high school or college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.400 --> 01:35:44.480
and their lived experience is just very different&nbsp;
from mine. My life doesn't lend itself to as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:44.480 --> 01:35:53.200
of that now as it would when I was 16, 17,&nbsp;
18, 20, early 20s. So I'm not sure how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:53.200 --> 01:35:58.280
model what they've achieved, in other words.
Sheila Heen: Part of what you're making me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:58.280 --> 01:36:06.200
think about is a conversation that I had with my&nbsp;
youngest sister who — I got married young. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:06.200 --> 01:36:16.280
I look back now and I'm like, "Why did I think&nbsp;
I had any business getting married at 26?" And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:16.280 --> 01:36:21.160
so I'm one of those people who have been married&nbsp;
forever. And I think my husband John and I grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:21.160 --> 01:36:37.880
up together in so many ways. My youngest sister&nbsp;
got married at 38, and when she met her husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:37.880 --> 01:36:42.360
well, at first she friend zoned him and&nbsp;
he wasn't a contender. And then suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:42.360 --> 01:36:47.947
she had this epiphany of like, "Oh, he's&nbsp;
the right person standing in front of me."&nbsp;

01:36:47.947 --> 01:36:50.960
Tim Ferriss: He was like, "I can wait. I&nbsp;
have the patience of an insect," as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:50.960 --> 01:36:56.800
of — "The praying mantis will wait."
Sheila Heen: That's right. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:56.800 --> 01:37:02.360
right. We actually commissioned&nbsp;
a custom song for their wedding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:02.360 --> 01:37:11.947
and it was all about Stacy's big epiphany and how&nbsp;
patient Dan had been. I know you'll come around.&nbsp;

01:37:11.947 --> 01:37:13.707
Tim Ferriss: Good for Dan.
Sheila Heen: Yeah, exactly.&nbsp;

01:37:13.707 --> 01:37:17.400
Tim Ferriss: Good for both of them, I guess.
Sheila Heen: Good for both of them. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:17.400 --> 01:37:21.200
real time, it didn't take that long. It&nbsp;
felt like forever to him. But he then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:21.800 --> 01:37:32.080
proposed quite quickly. My parents were&nbsp;
visiting. My sister also lives in Boston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:32.080 --> 01:37:41.920
and she left the room and he suddenly turned to&nbsp;
them and said, "I just wanted to let you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:41.920 --> 01:37:48.760
how much I am in love with your daughter, and I&nbsp;
want to ask her to marry me and spend the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:48.760 --> 01:37:58.040
my life with her." And my mother's like, "Well, I&nbsp;
have some follow-up questions." My father's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:58.040 --> 01:38:04.000
"Be quiet." He said to me later, "I wanted to&nbsp;
leap across the island and kiss him. Yes, we love&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:04.000 --> 01:38:09.720
you. We think you're awesome." Which reflects&nbsp;
something about each of my parents. But Stacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:09.720 --> 01:38:15.440
in the meantime was out of the room. And so she&nbsp;
then walks back in the room, this is a completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:15.440 --> 01:38:18.320
out-of-the-blue topic of conversation.
Tim Ferriss: They've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:18.320 --> 01:38:22.080
the secret caucus complete.
Sheila Heen: And suddenly it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:22.080 --> 01:38:26.680
"Well, okay, I'm going to run down and get a&nbsp;
bottle of champagne," kind of thing. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:26.680 --> 01:38:32.320
they weren't officially engaged yet, but it&nbsp;
was on the table that they were more or less,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:32.320 --> 01:38:35.800
and they had been dating for less than a year.
Tim Ferriss: How long had they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:35.800 --> 01:38:41.387
known each other though?
Sheila Heen: About a year.&nbsp;

01:38:41.387 --> 01:38:44.260
Tim Ferriss: Oh, all right. A year&nbsp;
before that or it was a year total?&nbsp;

01:38:44.260 --> 01:38:50.240
Sheila Heen: Yeah. Well, they met through a mutual&nbsp;
friend at a party. The friend had called Dan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:50.240 --> 01:38:53.400
say, "You need to break up with whoever you're&nbsp;
going out with, because I'm going to introduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:53.400 --> 01:38:57.227
you to someone tonight."
Tim Ferriss: Wow.&nbsp;

01:38:57.227 --> 01:38:58.360
Sheila Heen: But she didn't know.
Tim Ferriss: Hope they get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:58.360 --> 01:39:01.080
bouquet of flowers every year.
Sheila Heen: Oh, yeah. They have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:01.080 --> 01:39:03.987
milked that for all it's worth.
Tim Ferriss: As they should.&nbsp;

01:39:03.987 --> 01:39:11.840
Sheila Heen: As they should. And so they knew&nbsp;
each other for a few months before she figured&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:11.840 --> 01:39:18.360
it out. And then they were dating in earnest.&nbsp;
And then this is probably nine months after that.&nbsp;

01:39:18.360 --> 01:39:24.320
So after all the toasts, she and I went for&nbsp;
a run together, and she said, I said, "How&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:24.320 --> 01:39:32.280
are you feeling?" And she's, "It's just amazing,&nbsp;
and I can hardly believe it." And also I'm still&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:32.280 --> 01:39:38.880
waiting to find out what's wrong with him. And my&nbsp;
reaction was, "Well, five years from now, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:38.880 --> 01:39:44.200
going to have a much more robust answer for that&nbsp;
question of what's wrong with him. But the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:44.200 --> 01:39:51.040
question is, are they going to be deal breakers&nbsp;
for you? And what would the deal breakers be?"&nbsp;

01:39:52.160 --> 01:39:58.200
And so Tim, I'm curious, given your experience,&nbsp;
partly what you're listening for in the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:58.200 --> 01:40:05.960
stages of dating is are there deal breakers here&nbsp;
for me, not because I'm judging this other person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:05.960 --> 01:40:09.520
but because I just know that that combination's&nbsp;
not going to work or whatever. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:09.520 --> 01:40:15.720
one of them sounds like it's endless processing.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, endless processing would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:15.720 --> 01:40:24.960
one. And I want to own my part obviously in some&nbsp;
of this, in the sense that I'm sure I contributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:24.960 --> 01:40:31.680
to situations that were uncomfortable enough&nbsp;
for my significant other that she felt the need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:31.680 --> 01:40:38.480
to process. However, I do think she also leans&nbsp;
that direction in terms of communication. So yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:38.480 --> 01:40:50.280
endless processing or a primary project being&nbsp;
processing that is expected to be a dominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:50.280 --> 01:40:55.520
feature of the relationship. Necessary&nbsp;
for sure. But a dominant feature of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:55.520 --> 01:41:00.960
relationship? No, I think that it shouldn't&nbsp;
feel that hard. And if it does, chances are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:00.960 --> 01:41:07.920
there are things that are probably irreconcilable&nbsp;
differences. So that would be one deal breaker.&nbsp;

01:41:07.920 --> 01:41:13.200
Other deal breakers? If somebody throws&nbsp;
sharp elbows, that's really what I'm very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:13.200 --> 01:41:20.840
wary of for a lot of reasons. But if somebody&nbsp;
throws sharp elbows, if they are vindictive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:22.840 --> 01:41:35.040
if they speak in a really aggressive, upset way&nbsp;
about their exes, those are all red flags for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:35.040 --> 01:41:40.760
not yellow. Those are red. And it's astonishing&nbsp;
what some people, and I'm grateful they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:40.760 --> 01:41:47.160
but what some people will volunteer on first&nbsp;
dates in terms of, "Oh, yeah, my ex did this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:47.160 --> 01:41:53.400
and I showed him by taking a torch and doing&nbsp;
this and this and this." And I'm like, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:53.400 --> 01:42:00.480
hope you enjoyed your chicken salad because this&nbsp;
has been swell. Thank you." So the sharp elbows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:00.480 --> 01:42:10.520
somebody who's in any way vindictive is a deal&nbsp;
breaker. I would say if someone is a monologuer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:10.520 --> 01:42:19.240
that's also deal breaker. I want somebody who&nbsp;
is a good listener, good asker of questions. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:19.240 --> 01:42:27.720
think men tend to do this a lot more than women,&nbsp;
the monologuing. But I've seen some exceptions.&nbsp;

01:42:27.720 --> 01:42:33.280
Sheila Heen: I've dated some exceptions.
Tim Ferriss: Monologuers, overtalkers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:33.280 --> 01:42:41.880
People who step on me or other people when&nbsp;
they are mid-thought to interject or one up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:41.880 --> 01:42:48.240
that gets exhausting. Those would be a&nbsp;
few that come to mind. I'm sure there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:48.240 --> 01:42:56.040
are others, but those are a few that&nbsp;
immediately leap to mind when you ask.&nbsp;

01:42:56.040 --> 01:43:01.160
Sheila Heen: Part of what you're describing&nbsp;
is something that the Gottman Institute would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:01.160 --> 01:43:08.280
reinforce, which is that what are our&nbsp;
implicit rules about conflict? And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:08.280 --> 01:43:17.800
the couples that have staying power tend to have&nbsp;
boundaries on, no matter how upset I am with you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:17.800 --> 01:43:23.080
there are some places that I don't go&nbsp;
because in a long-term partnership you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:23.080 --> 01:43:28.587
know each other so well that you also have&nbsp;
the capacity to really hurt the other person.&nbsp;

01:43:28.587 --> 01:43:30.120
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.
Sheila Heen: And part of because you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:30.120 --> 01:43:35.960
know what would hurt them? And you secretly think&nbsp;
it sometimes, especially when you're frustrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:35.960 --> 01:43:42.640
and that even in those moments, you don't&nbsp;
go somewhere that is gratuitously that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:42.640 --> 01:43:48.440
sharp elbow or vindictiveness or meanness.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. You don't turn it into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:48.440 --> 01:43:53.600
Quentin Tarantino action sequence. It's not —
Sheila Heen: Yeah. And it's always amazing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:53.600 --> 01:43:55.480
me that they're actually —
Tim Ferriss: Kill Bill.&nbsp;

01:43:55.480 --> 01:43:58.107
Sheila Heen: Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: — all of a sudden.&nbsp;

01:43:58.107 --> 01:44:03.016
Sheila Heen: Suddenly, you didn't know&nbsp;
it, but I was wearing that yellow suit —&nbsp;

01:44:03.016 --> 01:44:04.560
Tim Ferriss: The yellow jumpsuit.
Sheila Heen: — jumpsuit under my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:04.560 --> 01:44:15.840
pajamas tonight. So I think that the boundaries&nbsp;
that we each assume on how we handle the hardest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:15.840 --> 01:44:23.640
moments, those being shared boundaries, feels —
— those being shared boundaries feels like a wise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:24.920 --> 01:44:29.920
guideline to have for yourself. The other thing&nbsp;
that I was thinking about about your processing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:29.920 --> 01:44:39.360
point is that for me it feels like, boy, there's&nbsp;
a long period during which we're bumping into each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:39.360 --> 01:44:45.000
other and where there's some friction between&nbsp;
us, processing it to understand it better. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:45.000 --> 01:44:48.680
was going on with me that I did interrupt you&nbsp;
— which by the way, interrupting is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:50.560 --> 01:44:56.640
feedback things I give my husband because he's&nbsp;
a big interrupter, which makes me crazy, crazy.&nbsp;

01:44:57.880 --> 01:45:04.080
That means in part — see, I'm blaming him for this&nbsp;
— that I am an interrupter, because if I don't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:04.080 --> 01:45:10.880
my word in edgewise, I'm not going to get in.&nbsp;
I think as a woman and a professional in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:10.880 --> 01:45:18.640
of the spaces I'm in, being willing to speak up to&nbsp;
get into the conversation when there's a moment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:18.640 --> 01:45:27.520
do so has in many contexts served me pretty well,&nbsp;
and in other contexts and other relationships, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:27.520 --> 01:45:32.160
doing to other people exactly the thing that he&nbsp;
does to me, my husband does to me, that makes me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:32.160 --> 01:45:40.640
so want to kill him. The Kill Bill comes to mind.
I think the question of understanding, gosh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:40.640 --> 01:45:44.440
what's going on with me, when does it happen,&nbsp;
what's going on with him, what does it happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:44.440 --> 01:45:51.800
that kind of processing can be helpful.&nbsp;
Then there's a point where we've talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:51.800 --> 01:45:59.480
about it a hundred times, we've dissected it a&nbsp;
hundred times, and we're back doing the thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:59.480 --> 01:46:09.320
that triggers the reaction. It just is. It just&nbsp;
is. There's a point where you have to decide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:09.320 --> 01:46:15.560
and for me, the switch is he's changed as&nbsp;
much as he's going to change, or I've changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:15.560 --> 01:46:21.240
as much as I'm going to be able to change.
I do it maybe less often or he does it less&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:21.240 --> 01:46:28.600
often. I'll still do it sometimes, and misstep.&nbsp;
If I assume this person is not going to change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:28.600 --> 01:46:36.040
can I live with that? Can we each live with&nbsp;
that? If so, then it's easier to have the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:36.040 --> 01:46:42.400
"Let me just note, you did that thing again."&nbsp;
"Oh, you're right. I apologize." We can talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:42.400 --> 01:46:49.720
about it later if we need to and we can move on,&nbsp;
because we don't need to reprocess something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:49.720 --> 01:46:53.960
we have already processed and we're at the&nbsp;
limits of how much each of us is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:53.960 --> 01:47:02.360
able to change sometimes, in some moments.
Tim Ferriss: Totally. What occurred to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:02.360 --> 01:47:08.920
as you're talking also is that people like to&nbsp;
do what they're good at. The big strong guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:08.920 --> 01:47:19.160
like to go to the weight room. The flexy people&nbsp;
like to go do yoga. The debaters like to debate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:19.160 --> 01:47:27.147
and the people who are on some level good at, say,&nbsp;
emotional processing like to emotionally process.&nbsp;

01:47:27.147 --> 01:47:30.280
Sheila Heen: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: If that is part of their identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:30.280 --> 01:47:37.880
then you are signing up for that. I think I&nbsp;
somewhat knowingly, somewhat unknowingly, probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:37.880 --> 01:47:46.920
signed up for that, and she is very, very good at&nbsp;
it. It was, I think, a constitutional — meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:46.920 --> 01:47:52.160
personal constitution — mismatch of sorts.
Sheila Heen: Not a national Constitutional issue.&nbsp;

01:47:52.160 --> 01:47:57.000
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Not talking about the&nbsp;
Second Amendment here. Relax, everybody. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:57.000 --> 01:47:59.920
people like to do what they're good at,&nbsp;
and I need to be cautious about that too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:59.920 --> 01:48:06.720
right? Because my response to some of that would&nbsp;
often be to try to put her in a cattle chute to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:06.720 --> 01:48:14.080
get her into this logic puzzle where I knew I&nbsp;
would be better. I'd try to steer her into some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:14.080 --> 01:48:21.600
labyrinth of spreadsheets and analytics because&nbsp;
I'm better at that stuff, and that wasn't helpful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:21.600 --> 01:48:26.920
It was hugely counterproductive, I'm sure, at many&nbsp;
different points, but that's what I'm good at.&nbsp;

01:48:28.360 --> 01:48:37.160
More and more, I've tried to cultivate an&nbsp;
awareness of what I do because I am good at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:37.160 --> 01:48:41.560
said thing, whatever that is, which applies&nbsp;
to not just relationships, but also how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:41.560 --> 01:48:44.800
structure your day and your week and choose&nbsp;
your projects. Are you choosing these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:44.800 --> 01:48:49.760
because they're truly the most important, or are&nbsp;
you choosing these things because you happen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:49.760 --> 01:48:55.987
have some skill and therefore it's comfortable for&nbsp;
you to do these things? I think about that a lot.&nbsp;

01:48:55.987 --> 01:49:01.880
Sheila Heen: I just want to make an observation&nbsp;
about what you just said which was so insightful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:01.880 --> 01:49:07.560
and where you feel comfortable and what you're&nbsp;
good at. Because for some people — and I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:07.560 --> 01:49:10.920
know her, so I don't know if it's true of&nbsp;
her — but for some people, part of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:10.920 --> 01:49:19.600
makes them feel safe or secure or reassured in a&nbsp;
relationship is to rock the boat. I've had some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:19.600 --> 01:49:26.400
relationships like that, and rocking the boat so&nbsp;
that we have something to process helps me feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:26.400 --> 01:49:33.600
connected to you. It's redemonstrating&nbsp;
that you care, and that processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:33.600 --> 01:49:39.400
emotional processing, goes to a place that feels&nbsp;
comfortable to me and feels reassuring to me.&nbsp;

01:49:39.400 --> 01:49:44.320
In that place — if we go back to the three&nbsp;
positions, which is just, there's my perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:44.320 --> 01:49:50.080
which is obviously correct. Then there's the other&nbsp;
person's perspective, the second position. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:50.080 --> 01:49:55.720
they're inside their story, and their world, and&nbsp;
why they think they're correct. And then either&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:55.720 --> 01:50:00.320
both of us could step to the third position.&nbsp;
What would a neutral observer say about what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:00.320 --> 01:50:06.800
going on and what might help us get through this?
She's in the place where, "I want to be connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:06.800 --> 01:50:11.480
with you. We need to be ourselves connecting&nbsp;
in the first and second position," and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:11.480 --> 01:50:15.240
place where you go because you feel more&nbsp;
comfortable might be the third position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:15.240 --> 01:50:18.680
to step out, to be analytical about it,&nbsp;
to say, "Well, let me just step back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:18.680 --> 01:50:22.047
understand what's happening here, because if&nbsp;
I can understand what's happening here..."&nbsp;

01:50:22.047 --> 01:50:23.960
Tim Ferriss: That's exactly what I do.
Sheila Heen: "...then I have advice for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:23.960 --> 01:50:29.760
myself about where to go," and she's like,&nbsp;
"Don't leave me here." You're disconnecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:29.760 --> 01:50:32.840
to go to the balcony or the third position.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. You should do this for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:32.840 --> 01:50:36.960
a living. Kidding, kidding.
Sheila Heen: That's the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:36.960 --> 01:50:45.960
in which that processing also was not serving&nbsp;
its purpose for her, and was not where you felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:45.960 --> 01:50:54.720
you wanted to be or what she was asking of you.
Tim Ferriss: That resonates as very on-point. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:54.720 --> 01:51:01.360
would further say that I contributed to&nbsp;
that by, for most of our relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:01.360 --> 01:51:08.560
being very weak in terms of her love language&nbsp;
of words of affirmation. It would make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:08.560 --> 01:51:18.440
to me that with a deficit, with a lack&nbsp;
or a deficiency of words of affirmation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:18.440 --> 01:51:27.440
that one — could be male, female, doesn't matter&nbsp;
— would want to rock the boat to elicit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:27.440 --> 01:51:34.640
type of connection in processing, to get those&nbsp;
words of affirmation. I think I almost certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:34.640 --> 01:51:39.160
contributed to that very directly in that way.
Sheila Heen: Yeah. I have that same profile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:39.160 --> 01:51:44.320
That's a weakness for me also. I see it showing&nbsp;
up in my personal relationships as well as my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:44.320 --> 01:51:47.107
work relationships. I'm pushing myself more.
Tim Ferriss: Giving words of affirmation?&nbsp;

01:51:47.107 --> 01:51:50.240
Sheila Heen: Yeah. My family was&nbsp;
not a super demonstrative family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:50.240 --> 01:51:53.040
to feel the need to say it out loud.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, same.&nbsp;

01:51:53.040 --> 01:51:54.120
Sheila Heen: It's changing.
Tim Ferriss: How are you working on that? How do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:54.120 --> 01:52:00.400
you work on that? I'm curious. I mean, I have&nbsp;
improved. I'm still very much on training wheels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:00.400 --> 01:52:09.200
but I have made substantial improvement, I think.&nbsp;
How are you working on it, or what have you found&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:09.200 --> 01:52:16.080
helpful to work on, giving words of affirmation,&nbsp;
positive reinforcement? Because I did not grow up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:16.080 --> 01:52:23.760
with a whole lot of that, and very much learned to&nbsp;
do without it in say sports as well, and in skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.760 --> 01:52:29.600
development. I trained myself not to really&nbsp;
require it and to be intrinsically motivated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:29.600 --> 01:52:38.400
so I then expect that of most people, which is an&nbsp;
unfair expectation. It just doesn't map to reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:38.400 --> 01:52:42.640
very well. How have you worked on it?
Sheila Heen: Some of it is just with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:42.640 --> 01:52:49.640
conscious reminders to myself to do it.
Tim Ferriss: Just like a timer on your phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:49.640 --> 01:52:56.267
an index card with five lines, that you can pull&nbsp;
out and be like, "Hold on. John." No, I'm kidding.&nbsp;

01:52:56.267 --> 01:52:57.320
Sheila Heen: Well, you're kidding.
Tim Ferriss: That's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:57.320 --> 01:53:01.800
kind of thing that I might do.
Sheila Heen: Honest to God, yeah. It's also trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:01.800 --> 01:53:07.200
to get better at noticing in myself when I do feel&nbsp;
grateful for something somebody did. If I get an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:07.200 --> 01:53:13.560
email that I'm just like, "Oh, my God, that is&nbsp;
so helpful," just to remember to say it out loud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:13.560 --> 01:53:19.080
The other thing is to just shoot them back a note&nbsp;
to say, "Thank you so much for taking the time it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:19.080 --> 01:53:26.200
took to send this to me, because it's incredibly&nbsp;
helpful," and that's all it needs to be. I also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.200 --> 01:53:35.240
noticed that I'm better at it in writing than&nbsp;
I am verbally, so that's okay if that's where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:35.240 --> 01:53:42.160
the training wheels are, to remember to just&nbsp;
text, to say when I notice something specific.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:42.160 --> 01:53:47.080
That's something we didn't talk about about&nbsp;
appreciation, which is just to seem genuine. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:47.080 --> 01:53:52.488
"Great job," isn't very genuine.
Tim Ferriss: Compelling.&nbsp;

01:53:52.488 --> 01:53:56.000
Sheila Heen: Being specific — yeah, compelling.&nbsp;
It doesn't sound necessarily sincere. Being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:56.000 --> 01:54:02.120
specific helps. Also just like, "Hey, I&nbsp;
particularly appreciated your note," or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:02.120 --> 01:54:09.560
"I thought your whatever had a really big&nbsp;
impact on me." I think the more I can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:09.560 --> 01:54:14.280
notice the small things that are specific,&nbsp;
the better. That's helped me. I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:14.280 --> 01:54:19.320
do you have other tips? Because I could use them.
Tim Ferriss: I was exaggerating a bit with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:19.320 --> 01:54:26.400
index card, but for a while — and I could&nbsp;
probably use with doing more of this — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:26.400 --> 01:54:32.600
would literally put in a calendar reminders, like,&nbsp;
"Say something nice to this person, this person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:32.600 --> 01:54:37.720
this person." There's always something I&nbsp;
can think of that is specific, and I just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:37.720 --> 01:54:46.280
for whatever malfunction, through being a&nbsp;
little nicer to myself, conditioning through my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:46.280 --> 01:54:52.160
upbringing, neglected to mention, then I would&nbsp;
mention. I'd literally put it in my calendar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:52.760 --> 01:55:01.600
and I found that helpful. I did find it helpful.
Sheila Heen: I also am appreciating your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:01.600 --> 01:55:09.360
connecting this to maybe what was going on&nbsp;
that caused the, "Hey, we need processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:09.360 --> 01:55:14.800
I need reassurance, because I'm not sure where&nbsp;
I stand or I felt overlooked or not seen,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:14.800 --> 01:55:22.640
et cetera, which part of what I'm learning is that&nbsp;
when I have somebody who is frustrated or upset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:22.640 --> 01:55:28.667
it is often that they are also feeling&nbsp;
underappreciated, and that's on me.&nbsp;

01:55:28.667 --> 01:55:38.760
Tim Ferriss: Totally. Let me ask you a bit&nbsp;
about giving feedback instead of receiving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:38.760 --> 01:55:46.360
and I know we've touched on this indirectly and&nbsp;
maybe even directly in this conversation, but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:46.360 --> 01:55:52.960
people who are in the supervisor, boss — or it&nbsp;
could be a significant other, but we've spent a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:52.960 --> 01:55:57.160
bunch of time on the personal. Let's talk about&nbsp;
the professional, even though they're two sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:57.160 --> 01:56:07.000
of the same coin. If you are giving feedback to&nbsp;
someone who tends to be sensitive, I'll just set&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:07.000 --> 01:56:12.520
the stage — this is not actually really the case&nbsp;
for me right now in any capacity, but it has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:12.520 --> 01:56:19.200
at various points with employees, with significant&nbsp;
others — what are your suggestions for giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:19.720 --> 01:56:30.400
feedback, and how to open that door hopefully&nbsp;
in a way that doesn't immediately provoke or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:30.400 --> 01:56:38.840
exacerbate a really defensive type of kickback?
Sheila Heen: One foundation thing is have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:38.840 --> 01:56:47.360
two of you had a conversation about how&nbsp;
they prefer to get feedback? That can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:47.360 --> 01:56:53.120
one of the most helpful and powerful things&nbsp;
to do, which is to sit down talk about, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:53.120 --> 01:56:57.960
what makes you feel appreciated?" Some people need&nbsp;
to hear the words. Other people don't really care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:57.960 --> 01:57:03.480
about the words, but the fact that you come&nbsp;
to them for advice with some of your toughest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:03.480 --> 01:57:11.680
problems, or you would like their input on your&nbsp;
proofreading, tells them they're valued. "What&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:11.680 --> 01:57:19.880
makes you feel appreciated? If I have coaching&nbsp;
for you, what's your advice to me on when and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:19.880 --> 01:57:29.280
how to give it? Do you have pet peeves about&nbsp;
feedback generally?" We all have pet peeves.&nbsp;

01:57:29.280 --> 01:57:35.600
That's a really interesting conversation to&nbsp;
have. "When you're triggered by feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:35.600 --> 01:57:43.120
how can I tell? How will I be able&nbsp;
to tell? It might be totally obvious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:43.120 --> 01:57:50.120
but sometimes people shut down, and I can't tell&nbsp;
whether you're taking it in or arguing in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:50.120 --> 01:57:57.720
head. When you are triggered or feeling defensive,&nbsp;
what advice do you have for me on what will help?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:59.720 --> 01:58:06.760
We have a little template that's like, "How to&nbsp;
get the best out of me," that each of us can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:06.760 --> 01:58:11.240
jot down some thoughts to and then talk about.
Tim Ferriss: How to get the best out of me. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:11.240 --> 01:58:14.907
that a template that is available online&nbsp;
or that we could put in the show notes?&nbsp;

01:58:14.907 --> 01:58:23.280
Sheila Heen: Yeah, on the Triad consulting&nbsp;
website, triadconsultinggroup.com. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:23.280 --> 01:58:32.387
a nav called "Help Yourself," and it's got&nbsp;
a bunch of templates, exercises, et cetera.&nbsp;

01:58:32.387 --> 01:58:38.320
Tim Ferriss: Great. We'll link to that.
Sheila Heen: Just having that conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:38.320 --> 01:58:46.720
up front means I don't have to guess at how to&nbsp;
give you feedback because already told me, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:46.720 --> 01:58:54.960
hopefully I've taken some notes which I keep handy&nbsp;
to remind myself. I can refer back, like, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:54.960 --> 01:58:59.720
I had a couple of thoughts about the presentation&nbsp;
last week, and I wonder when it would be helpful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:59.720 --> 01:59:06.360
to chat about it a little bit," or whatever.
The second thing is that really the fastest way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:06.360 --> 01:59:12.600
to change a feedback culture and to help people&nbsp;
be more receptive is to become a good receiver&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:12.600 --> 01:59:20.200
yourself, and be soliciting and eliciting feedback&nbsp;
from others. To assume every conversation, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:20.200 --> 01:59:25.240
when I think I'm pretty clearly the giver here,&nbsp;
I'm probably going to end up being a receiver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:25.240 --> 01:59:29.000
because what they're going to say is, "Well,&nbsp;
the reason I did that is you were so unclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:29.000 --> 01:59:33.480
about what you wanted," or whatever.&nbsp;
I have to assume there are things I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:33.480 --> 01:59:38.880
contributed to the situation that are going to&nbsp;
be part of the conversation, even when for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:38.880 --> 01:59:46.640
the primary purpose is to tell you what I&nbsp;
think you could do differently or better.&nbsp;

01:59:46.640 --> 01:59:54.960
There's a question that we use a lot that I think&nbsp;
is incredibly helpful just in building a habit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:54.960 --> 01:59:59.840
integrating feedback into daily life, which is&nbsp;
not, "Hey, do you have any feedback for me,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:59.840 --> 02:00:06.520
which we've talked about is a terrible question,&nbsp;
well-intended but terrible. Particularly from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:06.520 --> 02:00:12.840
leader, because giving feedback up feels very&nbsp;
risky and fraught. If you are in a position&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:12.840 --> 02:00:19.160
of leadership, you are impacting more and more&nbsp;
people, and fewer and fewer of them are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:19.160 --> 02:00:23.440
to take the chance to tell you about it. You've&nbsp;
got to actually have some pretty advanced skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:23.440 --> 02:00:29.920
in receiving feedback and inviting it.
One way you can do that is to ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:29.920 --> 02:00:35.040
"What's one thing? What's one thing that I'm&nbsp;
doing or maybe failing to do that you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:35.040 --> 02:00:38.800
is getting in the way, or what's one thing that,&nbsp;
if I could change it, would make a difference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:38.800 --> 02:00:43.720
you? Or what's one thing that, in our Monday&nbsp;
morning meeting, we could change to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:43.720 --> 02:00:48.200
more efficient, because I know people&nbsp;
are flagging, their energy's flagging?"&nbsp;

02:00:48.200 --> 02:00:51.800
That's a question that you can toss&nbsp;
off while you're walking down the hall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:51.800 --> 02:00:56.600
and it lowers the stakes. It's very clear you're&nbsp;
asking for coaching. You're looking for something&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:56.600 --> 02:01:03.640
to improve. You're also signaling, "By the way,&nbsp;
I expect you to be receptive to coaching also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:03.640 --> 02:01:06.760
because I'm going to demonstrate I&nbsp;
value it. I assume I'm still learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:06.760 --> 02:01:11.187
and I expect that you're still learning too."
Tim Ferriss: One thing. One thing.&nbsp;

02:01:11.187 --> 02:01:14.640
Sheila Heen: What's one thing?
Tim Ferriss: It's funny how one thing at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:14.640 --> 02:01:18.600
put in sequence, how much that can do.
Sheila Heen: It's not is there anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:18.600 --> 02:01:21.520
because if you ask is there anything, people&nbsp;
will be like, "Oh, no, you're great. We love&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:21.520 --> 02:01:23.800
working with you." You have to assume there is.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. That's kind of like my,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:23.800 --> 02:01:25.987
"If you had to cut 20 percent, what&nbsp;
would you cut?" Not "Would you cut?"&nbsp;

02:01:25.987 --> 02:01:28.168
Sheila Heen: Yeah, that's one of the&nbsp;
reasons that's such a great question.&nbsp;

02:01:28.168 --> 02:01:34.480
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's forced. It applies some&nbsp;
very clear constraints. The option of not naming&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:34.480 --> 02:01:41.120
something is not implicit in that question.
Sheila Heen: That's fair. Do you get people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:41.120 --> 02:01:44.560
who ignore the question anyway?
Tim Ferriss: No, because they make&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:44.560 --> 02:01:52.800
terrible proofreaders. Generally my friends know.&nbsp;
These are people I've known for a while typically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:52.800 --> 02:01:57.000
so they know exactly what they're signing&nbsp;
up for, and they know what type of feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:57.000 --> 02:02:00.427
I have given them in the past also.
Sheila Heen: Right, so it's reciprocal?&nbsp;

02:02:00.427 --> 02:02:07.160
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, for sure.&nbsp;
They have a very good idea of exactly what I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:07.160 --> 02:02:14.280
looking for and they'll ask clarifying questions&nbsp;
if need be, but what is one thing, dot-dot-dot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:14.280 --> 02:02:20.960
is a really useful heuristic for getting the&nbsp;
type of feedback that may not be forthcoming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:20.960 --> 02:02:28.240
especially if you are at the top of the pyramid&nbsp;
or in the hierarchy in a leadership position.&nbsp;

02:02:28.240 --> 02:02:32.440
Well, Sheila, we've covered a lot. What have we&nbsp;
not covered? Is there anything that you would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:32.440 --> 02:02:41.320
to discuss before we begin to wind to a close?&nbsp;
Have we neglected anything that pops to mind?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:41.320 --> 02:02:47.160
We've certainly gone through a lot of different&nbsp;
scenarios. We've gone through the interpersonal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:47.160 --> 02:02:51.800
we've gone through intimate relationships,&nbsp;
we've gone through professional environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:51.800 --> 02:02:57.440
Is there any particular point that comes&nbsp;
to mind or anything you'd like to add,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:57.440 --> 02:03:02.680
before we begin to land the plane?
Sheila Heen: What's in my head is maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:02.680 --> 02:03:09.080
a follow-on to what you just said about the one&nbsp;
thing and being in leadership, which is, so when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:09.080 --> 02:03:16.640
we were working on the feedback book, part&nbsp;
of the challenge is that the need to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:16.640 --> 02:03:21.960
feedback is a felt problem. I have feedback&nbsp;
for you. I'm carrying it around with myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:21.960 --> 02:03:24.680
because I don't know how to give it to you,&nbsp;
or I've tried to give it to you and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:24.680 --> 02:03:32.000
not taking it. If there's a book that would help&nbsp;
me do that, I would buy that book, and could use&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:32.000 --> 02:03:38.680
some help. The need to receive feedback from other&nbsp;
people who are carrying it around and not giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:38.680 --> 02:03:47.387
it to us is not necessarily a felt problem. I'm&nbsp;
oblivious to it. I have mixed feelings about it.&nbsp;

02:03:47.387 --> 02:03:50.627
Tim Ferriss: It's like carbon&nbsp;
monoxide, the invisible killer.&nbsp;

02:03:50.627 --> 02:03:53.360
Sheila Heen: That's right. It is the&nbsp;
invisible killer. Actually that's a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:53.360 --> 02:04:00.947
good metaphor for it. Do I want a carbon&nbsp;
monoxide detector? I do. I know I should.&nbsp;

02:04:00.947 --> 02:04:04.960
Tim Ferriss: You do.
Sheila Heen: It's also moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:04.960 --> 02:04:12.080
from a push model of learning — I decide what you&nbsp;
need to learn and I push you to learn it — to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:12.080 --> 02:04:19.880
pull model of learning, which is I'm curious what&nbsp;
you think I have to learn or could improve, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:19.880 --> 02:04:26.827
is very much your stance, Tim. It's very relaxing&nbsp;
to be with someone who has that stance, right?&nbsp;

02:04:26.827 --> 02:04:30.560
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. It's also — and I think&nbsp;
your book does a great job of this — I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:30.560 --> 02:04:36.120
it is a learnable skill. I learned this&nbsp;
through skill acquisition, where for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:36.120 --> 02:04:45.960
I would need to have to turn laypeople,&nbsp;
non-coaches, into coaches. To do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:45.960 --> 02:04:52.400
you have to elicit with lots of very specific&nbsp;
questions and very well-thought-through questions.&nbsp;

02:04:52.400 --> 02:04:58.680
For instance, if you want a native speaker to help&nbsp;
you learn your target language, that is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:58.680 --> 02:05:06.320
a very big ask and very challenging if they're&nbsp;
not trained teachers. If you go up to a native&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:06.320 --> 02:05:09.880
English speaker and you're like, "Tell me the&nbsp;
difference between anything and something," or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:09.880 --> 02:05:17.000
"When do you use an versus the when it's a&nbsp;
new person being introduced," to a sense. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:17.000 --> 02:05:21.320
native English speakers would fail the test of&nbsp;
English as a foreign language, or do very poorly.&nbsp;

02:05:21.320 --> 02:05:28.120
It would be so confusing, because what is&nbsp;
reflexive is not something they can explain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:28.120 --> 02:05:38.560
I think I've developed that stance through a lot&nbsp;
of repetitions with mostly different skills. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:38.560 --> 02:05:43.840
is not something that came intuitively, but&nbsp;
it is something that you can pick up very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:43.840 --> 02:05:48.880
very quickly with the right toolkit.
Sheila Heen: With that toolkit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:48.880 --> 02:05:58.160
you become an incredibly skilled and fast learner.&nbsp;
I think that's a big aha for me, which is, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:58.160 --> 02:06:05.480
gosh, I can learn from anybody. They don't have to&nbsp;
be a skilled giver, because I can help with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:05.480 --> 02:06:10.440
to hear and understand what they're trying to tell&nbsp;
me, and then I can figure out what to make of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:10.440 --> 02:06:16.120
and what I want to take away and leave behind."&nbsp;
The last thing on this front is that as leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:16.120 --> 02:06:22.880
sometimes people will say, "Well, I don't&nbsp;
really know what I should be working on."&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:23.960 --> 02:06:29.000
My answer to them is like, "Well, you know&nbsp;
who knows? Pretty much everybody else."&nbsp;

02:06:29.000 --> 02:06:36.120
Everybody around you has a list of the things you&nbsp;
do to make it harder for them to do their job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:36.120 --> 02:06:43.280
It's a list they pass amongst each other about&nbsp;
you. It becomes the conventional wisdom about you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:43.280 --> 02:06:48.800
what's great about you and what's hard about&nbsp;
working with you. If you want to know what's on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:48.800 --> 02:06:53.880
that list, you have to ask, and you have to ask&nbsp;
in a way that persuades them that you actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:53.880 --> 02:06:58.480
want to know and you'll take it seriously.&nbsp;
You can't promise that you can change it or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:58.480 --> 02:07:04.120
you would change it, but at least listening&nbsp;
for the themes that you hear from different&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:04.120 --> 02:07:08.200
people at different times will help identify&nbsp;
the things that, "Oh, yeah, that's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:08.200 --> 02:07:15.120
something that's not helping in this context."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. This topic of feedback in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:15.120 --> 02:07:21.400
Thanks for the Feedback: The Science and Art of&nbsp;
Receiving Feedback Well, it's a bit of a Trojan&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:21.400 --> 02:07:27.240
horse for a bunch of things, as you mentioned,&nbsp;
right? If you want to be a super learner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:27.240 --> 02:07:34.400
it doesn't just apply to interacting with&nbsp;
your boss at work. It doesn't just apply to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:34.400 --> 02:07:39.080
your direct reports at work. It doesn't just&nbsp;
apply to your significant other. It actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:39.080 --> 02:07:45.320
applies in a million different contexts.&nbsp;
How can you elicit the feedback that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:45.320 --> 02:07:51.840
most help you to take constructive next steps? I&nbsp;
mean, it applies to everything. To that extent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:51.840 --> 02:07:59.880
it's very much a meta skill, that is transferable&nbsp;
across a million different disciplines.&nbsp;

